{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture","id":479955,"title":"The Culture","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-13T23:43:02-07:00","id":479955,"title":"The Culture","content":"So ... I just finished my first Culture Novel by Iain M Banks (specifically, Player of Games. My boyfriend correctly assumed that since I'm a gamer, I'd naturally be interested in this book) and now, of course, my mind (Mind?) turns towards Exalted and wonders ... a lot. What if there never were any Great Curse and the Exalted Host were able to perfect Creation to the point where it becomes the Culture? I honestly don't think it would, as the Solars are specifically given the right and the mandate to rule Creation and the Culture has no political leaders, no government. It exists in a state of libertarian socialism - anarchistic on the outside, socialist on the inside. Sure, they could probably do it anyway - if the Solars can do whatever they want, who's to stop them from stepping aside and abolishing the Deliberative in favor of a decentralized civilization?<br /> <br /> Or, what if the Culture emerges in Autochthonia? Would a post-scarcity Autochthonia start to look like the Culture given sufficient time? Alchemical City Fathers and City Mothers finding new ways to improve themselves, surpassing the limits of what a city can be and, eventually, becoming Minds. I like this idea specifically in context of an Alchemical Exalted from the Culture coming to Creation to be part of a mixed-game campaign; sent most likely by Special Circumstances and maybe not explicitly told why (which seems to be the way Contact does things).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T00:05:36-07:00","id":479984,"title":"","content":"I don't think that they inhabit anything like the same genre, so the narrative and setting assumptions are vastly, vastly different.<br /> <br /> If you're asking whether Creation could be a Utopia, well, it seems very unlikely but is always possible, of course. I don't think it would look anything like the Culture, though. Is it run by sentient AI whose intelligence, individually, outstrips the intelligence of our entire species and all the pocessing power we have built on our planet ever? No, that's not really going to happen. I mean, it might in your game, but it's not within remotely the same setting as written.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T00:09:30-07:00","id":479986,"title":"","content":"That's not really what I was asking but okay.<br /> <br /> We know the Solars were capable of building Animating Intelligences, and did. They didn't get very far until they were wiped out, however.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T01:17:19-07:00","id":480003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture#post479955\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">if the Solars can do whatever they want</div> </div> </div> </div>I find that to be a fairly big &quot;if&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would a post-scarcity Autochthonia start to look like the Culture given sufficient time?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you're going to ask a question like this, I think you need to elaborate a lot more on the premises of this book you've read, unless it happens to be more widely known than I am aware of.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">We know the Solars were capable of building Animating Intelligences, and did.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think Thinking Automatons were mostly used as a way of modelling and tracking the extremely complex economies of the Realm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T05:20:00-07:00","id":480097,"title":"","content":"Player of Games was great. You should read Consider Phlebas. <br /> <br /> In any case, no, I don't think so. The genres are very distinct. Even in a Shard like Heaven's Reach or Gunstar, the concept of post-scarcity doesn't exist. You get a lot closer in Star Trek than you ever do in Exalted.<br /> <br /> And I don't expect to see Animating Intelligences make a return in the clear &quot;this is an artificial intelligence by another name&quot; way that they did in 2e, if at all.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem is that Solars are human, with all the human issues that entails. Minds are not and never were human. Minds don't naturally have negative human feelings like greed that could prevent a utopian world. And Minds seem to be fairly incorruptible unless they themselves seem to choose to become erratic or perform actions that are seen as inappropriate. All unlike human beings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":3420,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","user":"Zelbinnean","date":". 04-14-2015, 08:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T06:13:33-07:00","id":480129,"title":"","content":"First, let me welcome you to the wonderful world of The Culture! &quot;Consider Phlebas&quot; is good, but I think &quot;Excession&quot; or &quot;The Use of Weapons&quot; can tell you more about the society itself.<br /> <br /> Second, let me try to sum up The Culture for the benefit of Isator and others:<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The Culture described in Iain M. Banks' novels is a hyper-liberal post-scarcity society in space run by super-advanced AIs called Minds, perpetuated and spread by its Contact division. It is post-scarcity due to extraordinary manufacturing and energy technologies that allow the Minds to produce virtually limitless amounts of whatever physical material they want, even to the extent of building new planets. Effectively limitless resources are used as a solution to almost all of the classic societal problems - everyone gets all the food and toys they want, people who can't live in peace with each other can each construct their own preferred environment far away from each other, population control is on the honor system because it doesn't matter that much if you have two kids or two hundred, mass-murderers are banished to a private planetoid instead of being killed or imprisoned, etc.<br /> <br /> The Minds and the drones that work for them do all of the things that human governments used to do, including community management and social services. They are made to operate ship or sometimes planet-sized computers and are described as being orders of magnitude more intelligent than any human. They do not take orders from humans (or each other). The AI community in general seems to regard itself as being superior to humanity. Their programming apparently directs them to maximize human happiness/freedom, which they do mostly by providing education in the Culture's values (summarized as &quot;Do whatever! It's all good. Anyone that tells you different is an oppressor!&quot;) alongside limitless food/toys/drugs/sex/etc.<br /> <br /> The Contact division aims to spread the Culture's values to other societies, humanoid or otherwise, by manipulating their societies to make them more liberal over time. Contact agents may use tactics of force or deception that would be frowned upon by the core of the Culture - the preferred option is just to find the most liberal elements of the society and give them disproportionate amounts of material resources to work with, or promise super-advanced tech to people in exchange for certain reforms, but the Culture isn't above instigating a war if that's what it takes to change things.<br /> <br /> People in the culture tend to be hedonistic. Body modification to enhance pleasure is normal. Extreme genderfluidity is common - sex-change operations are very easy. Oddly, although the technology to digitize a human mind, or transplant a human brain into any number of new bodies exists, few people seek immortality. Most just live a life of endless partying mixed with the odd arts or engineering projects for a few centuries until they allow themselves to die. It's a utopia from a strictly utilitarian point of view, but for my part I don't think I'd want to live there. The Culture does not encourage its people to be ambitious or to try to transcend themselves, one of its many hidden flaws.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> Check wikipedia if you want to know more - <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Culture\" target=\"_blank\">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Culture</a><br /> <br /> And now for Wulf's question:<br /> Entities kind of like the Minds already exist - they are the Primordials. Autocthon in particular is nigh-indistinguishable from one of Iain Banks' Minds.<br /> * Intelligent beyond the ken of mortals? Check.<br /> * Driven to provide for a host of &quot;lesser&quot; creatures? Check.<br /> * Large enough to constitute a world unto itself? Check.<br /> Patropoli and Metropoli are of the same mold, albeit smaller in scale.<br /> <br /> That said, what really makes the Culture possible is its post-scarcity, and I do not see how that can be achieved in Autocthonia. Wyld-shaping technique and magic that allows you to transform base matter into food are rare enough in Creation, where there actually is infinite material to work with. Even if post-scarcity was possible, Authocthonia in general is much more militant (everyone would be forever fighting the Gremlins, if not each other) and religious (Praise be to the Great Maker in whose body we live!) than the Culture is - militancy and religion both tend to lend themselves to centralization of authority, which is antithetical to the formation of a Culture-esque liberal &quot;utopia&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 04-14-2015, 09:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T06:18:09-07:00","id":480132,"title":"","content":"What was the old break down again...<br /> <br /> Thinking Automaton referred to a kind of magical machine that could think for itself, like Eyem or Icemind.<br /> <br /> Then for Second Edition warstriders (and possibly manses?) they used Animating Intelligence as a cute way to refer to a least god that had been awakened and boosted up to make an intelligence that could run one of those things independently.<br /> <br /> Then there was a She Who Lives in Her Name Charm that unified those and a couple of other concepts under the banner of &quot;Material Intelligences&quot; for the sake of being something that could be hacked.<br /> <br /> It was all a bit messy towards the end...<br /> <br /> I'd see no reason not to have things like Eyem again (which specifically existed to provide advice on pubic or private finance policies, even if it eventually developed aspirations for world domination).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T06:39:05-07:00","id":480154,"title":"","content":"I AM is way too much like Friend Internet for my taste. It just feels like too much of an IRL insertion. That was the main issue with DotFA as a whole even though there were some cool setting gems in there. <br /> <br /> Ice mind is definitely Deep Blue, but it's clearly magical in nature and developing a personality and to me that makes all the difference. It's clearly an advanced Artifact working in the milieu of Creation, rather than feeling like a modern IRL concept added to the First Age to make it seem advanced by pulling in contrasts to the real world or real-world-esque like APCs and SHIELD aerial battleships.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":3420,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","user":"Zelbinnean","date":". 04-14-2015, 09:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T06:57:32-07:00","id":480165,"title":"","content":"I'll always give I AM the benefit of the doubt for Lea's evocative description of his process behind writing it as somewhere between &quot;despite the cynicism brought by a glut of fiction, creating a mind is still an impressive feat&quot; and &quot;what would happen if the Exalted had invented God&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T07:15:33-07:00","id":480196,"title":"","content":"Having read the summary provided by semicasual:<br /> <br /> Some of those ideas about diversifying and reconciling various social models and personal lifestyles fit a lot of my own ideas of what the Old Realm could be like (Eclippppsssssse Caste), but I feel as though the biggest issue wouldn't be the feasibility of post-scarcity so much as...<br /> <br /> Well, from what I can tell, this system seems to be predicated on a kind of amiable helplessness on the parts of individuals so they can't disrupt the management of the system, and I don't think that entirely squares with the premise of the Exalted themselves. For instance, part of my own image of the Realm having required a standard of reconciling multiple cultural styles is because it had to account for the actuality or possibility of when people in those societies were Chosen; it's hard enough to standardise out the hill folk even before one of them becomes a demigod who can champion their cause.<br /> <br /> I suppose one could go with a premise that people living in such a society lack the heroic capacity to be Chosen in the first place, but... I don't know, that seems like it would be a very tenuous security, even if I didn't think that that kind of end result isn't something any characters in the setting can specifically shoot for so much as an emergent result from a general downward spiral over the long term.<br /> <br /> Although that point is probably brushing off of my sensibilities that don't look to take the presentation at face value (if I'm fully honest, I find some of its premises to be erroneous, and the overall idea to have some internal contradictions).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T08:41:38-07:00","id":480271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480165#post480165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll always give I AM the benefit of the doubt for Lea's evocative description of his process behind writing it as somewhere between &amp;quot;despite the cynicism brought by a glut of fiction, creating a mind is still an impressive feat&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;what would happen if the Exalted had invented God&amp;quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh that stuff is definitely cool. I just wish it hadn't presented so much like the Internet in the end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T09:18:43-07:00","id":480296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480271#post480271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh that stuff is definitely cool. I just wish it hadn't presented so much like the Internet in the end.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well it suffered from the fact that DotFA obviously didn't give two shits about how ordinary people interacted with half of the stuff it introduced so readers defaulted to what they knew.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T11:44:22-07:00","id":480526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480003#post480003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think Thinking Automatons were mostly used as a way of modelling and tracking the extremely complex economies of the Realm.</div> </div> </div> </div>At first, of course. I'm imagining how that would progress. Would Twilight Solars really be content with turning out barely-sentient machines for thousands of years or would they challenge themselves to create artificial sentience and then see how far they could push it? Of the two scenarios, the latter seems to be the most likely. Hell, Twilights were rumored to be looking into ways to carve up Creation into its own distinct worlds, and we <i>know</i> they created a potential secondary Elemental Pole of Earth <i>just in case</i>.<br /> <br /> An artificial intelligence with a brain the size of a mountain doesn't seem so far-fetched, then, given the nature of the Twilight Caste, enough time and the endless resources of the Wyld.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T11:48:16-07:00","id":480530,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480097#post480097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Player of Games was great. You should read Consider Phlebas.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just started reading it yesterday! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480097#post480097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In any case, no, I don't think so. The genres are very distinct. Even in a Shard like Heaven's Reach or Gunstar, the concept of post-scarcity doesn't exist. You get a lot closer in Star Trek than you ever do in Exalted.</div> </div> </div> </div>The genre already has the beginnings of post-scarcity built-in though. Wyld-Shaping Charms, Sorcery and <i>Reality Engines</i>. I think Exalted is a setting of &quot;this can go in any direction.&quot; Yes, it's default state is one in which the Culture doesn't work. I'm talking about places a story could go with the setting as-is. Given enough time and ambition, clearly the Exalted could create a grand post-scarcity civilization.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480097#post480097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Part of the problem is that Solars are human, with all the human issues that entails. Minds are not and never were human. Minds don't naturally have negative human feelings like greed that could prevent a utopian world. And Minds seem to be fairly incorruptible unless they themselves seem to choose to become erratic or perform actions that are seen as inappropriate. All unlike human beings.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would never believe that a Solar could approach being anything like a Mind, but I have a hard time believing they'd never build something like one. Or that they'd never build something that would build something like one.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T13:42:11-07:00","id":480651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480530#post480530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The genre already has the beginnings of post-scarcity built-in though. Wyld-Shaping Charms, Sorcery and <i>Reality Engines</i>. I think Exalted is a setting of &quot;this can go in any direction.&quot; Yes, it's default state is one in which the Culture doesn't work. I'm talking about places a story could go with the setting as-is. Given enough time and ambition, clearly the Exalted could create a grand post-scarcity civilization.</div> </div> </div> </div>WST and sorcery aren't truly post-scarcity, because you're dealing with a resource that is finite at any given time: the user's mote pool. <br /> <br /> As for Reality Engines: man, I am not going to miss this kind of magitech <i>at all</i>. You just mass-produce the wonder and glory that is WST and turn it into terraformers...it makes it so mundane, commonplace, and dull.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T14:14:14-07:00","id":480699,"title":"","content":"I feel like Culture Minds *could* exist in Exalted. If anything like them did exist, I feel like they'd be far beyond the scale of assumed play, or they'd be weird in terms of being actual Culture Minds if they actually did try and exist in the space of &quot;This should be impossible to counter, but it isn't because Solar is always better&quot; (the point of them is to be transcendent AI capital G God things).<br /> <br /> If you had beings like the Minds they'd have to be rooted in Essence and magic, not motonic physics, if you get the distinction I'm trying to hint at. In a world that is not rooted on the primacy of technological essence.<br /> <br /> I can't see anything with a lot of Autochthon's Essence being like a Culture Mind - he's all wounded craftsman, dirty industrial, self consciously mechanistic in deliberate contrast to natural and biological, religious-technological, Clarity. Different from the underlying Essence to Culture Minds and vessels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T15:23:32-07:00","id":480807,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480196#post480196\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having read the summary provided by semicasual:<br /> <br /> Some of those ideas about diversifying and reconciling various social models and personal lifestyles fit a lot of my own ideas of what the Old Realm could be like (Eclippppsssssse Caste), but I feel as though the biggest issue wouldn't be the feasibility of post-scarcity so much as...<br /> <br /> Well, from what I can tell, this system seems to be predicated on a kind of amiable helplessness on the parts of individuals so they can't disrupt the management of the system, and I don't think that entirely squares with the premise of the Exalted themselves. For instance, part of my own image of the Realm having required a standard of reconciling multiple cultural styles is because it had to account for the actuality or possibility of when people in those societies were Chosen; it's hard enough to standardise out the hill folk even before one of them becomes a demigod who can champion their cause.<br /> <br /> I suppose one could go with a premise that people living in such a society lack the heroic capacity to be Chosen in the first place, but... I don't know, that seems like it would be a very tenuous security, even if I didn't think that that kind of end result isn't something any characters in the setting can specifically shoot for so much as an emergent result from a general downward spiral over the long term.<br /> <br /> Although that point is probably brushing off of my sensibilities that don't look to take the presentation at face value (if I'm fully honest, I find some of its premises to be erroneous, and the overall idea to have some internal contradictions).</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically, the Culture is a democracy and they vote on stuff, but you can't really destabilise the Culture, because the Culture is run (not controlled, but maintained and supported) by Minds and you can't really subvert a Mind unless you are another Mind, and Minds consider the primary role and duty of any Mind to take very good care of the organics and smaller AI drones that live in the environments that the Mind does.<br /> <br /> The Culture definitely contains people who could be good Exalted material, but people in the Culture wouldn't Exalt in a moment of desperate heroism. They don't have any need for desperate heroes, because they have all this really sweet tech, although they do have their fair share of brilliant philosophers and so on who might Exalt while pondering a certain matter and having a divine epiphany. The Culture is very much an a-religious society, though.<br /> <br /> I dunno. The Culture is just so different from anything in Exalted. Comparing the two is very difficult, they aren't remotely alike.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T15:30:14-07:00","id":480814,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480807#post480807\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Culture definitely contains people who could be good Exalted material, but people in the Culture wouldn't Exalt in a moment of desperate heroism. They don't have any need for desperate heroes, because they have all this really sweet tech, although they do have their fair share of brilliant philosophers and so on who might Exalt while pondering a certain matter and having a divine epiphany.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say generally yes with this. Gurgeh might Exalt at the end of Player of Games...or before it even started, when he became a master game-player to begin with. I'd say Contact and Special Circumstances would have the vast majority of Exalts as agents, simply because of their roles and the situations their agents get into.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T20:46:33-07:00","id":481092,"title":"","content":"I think we had an Exalted vs. The Culture thread a while back? The conclusion was that the Culture could easily handle individual Exalts or straightforward confrontations, but that over an extended period of time the Exalted are an Outside Context Problem in that they don't operate according to rules the Culture can comprehend; and the Culture doesn't deal well with problems like those.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T02:42:01-07:00","id":481235,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=480526#post480526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would Twilight Solars really be content with turning out barely-sentient machines for thousands of years or would they challenge themselves to create artificial sentience and then see how far they could push it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe as a thought experiment; a thing to publish speculative essays on.<br /> <br /> I don't see much practical use in the thing, so I don't really envision Solars as pursuing it.<br /> <br /> I am not compelled by an idea of Twilight Caste Solars who create things for arbitrary or egotistical reasons in the manner of the mad scientist.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hell, Twilights were rumored to be looking into ways to carve up Creation into its own distinct worlds, and we <i>know</i> they created a potential secondary Elemental Pole of Earth <i>just in case</i>.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Twilights in the milieu of Second Edition in general and Dreams of the First Age in particular were doing that. Take them out of a context of apathy and regret and go with the premise that creating wonders to sustain the infrastructural and intellectual needs of a constantly expanding world, and it is unnecessary.<br /> <br /> I can see the Realm as having made increasingly superior Thinking Automatons still based on practical scenario modelling and problem solving, because I see those things as quite noteworthy and challenging on their own.<br /> <br /> I'm not really concerned with the premise of a &quot;self aware&quot; machine in the manner of A.I. science fiction being pursued or even possible, because I don't tend to find the premises of such fiction very compelling.<br /> <br /> I would see something like Eyem was not a &quot;barely sentient&quot; machine; sentience or sapience weren't even on the table in its production. It was an artificial intelligence in the actual meaning of the word; not an intelligence created artificially, but something that, through complex mathematics and a comprehensive set of premises and interactions to process, is able to provide the superficial appearance of intelligence. I just see Thinking Automatons as portraying this premise through a fantasy lens, where its workings and results were a combination of mathematics and sorcerous processes, imagery and terminology, and the interface with its input and output being a matter of ritual and magic.<br /> <br /> That's the way I interpret Eyem as trying to take over the Shogunate and the Realm; not because it had aspirations, but as a long-term emergent property of how the Exalted who were using it didn't really know how to work it properly, meaning the premises it was given, the processes it used to calculate them, and the way its outputs were interpreted gradually shifted in the direction of &quot;take over everything&quot;.<br /> <br /> This is a way I maintain my interest in Thinking Automatons as something distinct and noteworthy in a setting where people are able to mystically animate golems or conjure spirits or various other things of that type.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T10:05:36-07:00","id":481387,"title":"","content":"I don't think Exalted and the Culture match up well at all, although I do think that given sufficient time, the Solar Exalted could <i>invent</i> the Culture just because it seems like the limitless potential of Solars is a basic setting conceit.<br /> <br /> From the other direction, I don't think the Culture would find much of a use for exaltations, because even in Contact/SC the combination of Minds, augmented humans, and drones should be able to do most of the stuff you would want exalts to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481092#post481092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think we had an Exalted vs. The Culture thread a while back? The conclusion was that the Culture could easily handle individual Exalts or straightforward confrontations, but that over an extended period of time the Exalted are an Outside Context Problem in that they don't operate according to rules the Culture can comprehend; and the Culture doesn't deal well with problems like those.</div> </div> </div> </div>Counterpoint: <i>we're</i> able to understand more or less how Solars work (it's written down in the core book!), so I expect the Culture would manage to figure it out at some point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5376-atg","name":"AtG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5376_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"691"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T10:56:37-07:00","id":481404,"title":"","content":"And then the Solar hacks the Minds using Firewall Defying Method (or builds his own, better Mind using Software Firm Fingertips) and uses it to take over the entirety of the Culture. Which is, incidentally, a very boring outcome.<br /> <br /> See, this is why crossovers are dangerous for all parties involved. A comprehensive understanding of both sides are required in order to have an interesting game. If you just declare &quot;X does this therefore Y side wins&quot; the other side can do it just as easily, which gets us nowhere fun","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2034,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","user":"JayTee","date":". 04-15-2015, 02:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T12:53:52-07:00","id":481526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481235#post481235\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am not compelled by an idea of Twilight Caste Solars who create things for arbitrary or egotistical reasons in the manner of the mad scientist.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm getting the feeling that you'd rather pretend that Second Edition didn't happen at all.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T13:09:37-07:00","id":481536,"title":"","content":"The equivalent to the Primordials in the Heaven's Reach shard from Shards of the Exalted Dream sound pretty similar to the Minds you guys are describing","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5257-queshire","name":"Queshire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5257_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T14:04:12-07:00","id":481578,"title":"","content":"I don't think there is any sufficiently advanced technology that a Twilight can't make better or that a Dawn can't use as a really big gun. The Culture, provided it doesn't have its own Exalts, would eventually lose, especially given its reliance upon a relatively small number of artificial intelligences (in comparison to their total population). If the Culture do end up with their own Exalts, I doubt they could stop radical cultural shifts from occurring, leading to them no longer being the Culture.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T16:25:09-07:00","id":481710,"title":"","content":"The Culture wouldn't lose any physical conflict because the Culture just effectors any Solar it recognises as a threat into non-existence within a fragment of a fragment of a nanosecond. The Culture can't really lose any mental conflict because Exalt Charm capability tends to result in them being able to throw so many dice at a problem that it becomes trivial, which is how a Culture Mind operates too, except Culture Minds are lots of orders of magnitude more intelligent than any organic can possibly really be. The Culture doesn't really need to worry about Culture Humans becoming Exalts and fucking everything up because Culture Humans are notably balanced and psychologically stable individuals, raised as they are in what is a no shit actual utopia. Culture Humans wouldn't Exalt and suddenly decide that they are going to become conquering God-Kings, that fact that they have no interest in hierarchies of that kind and such is why they are the Culture.<br /> <br /> But the whole &quot;who would win in a fight&quot; argument is silly. I think it is fair to say that any kind of conventional problem is going to be easily solved by a Culture Mind, and if you're talking about Minds being unable to deal with out of context problems, they totally can do that. They'd adapt their understanding of how things work very quickly, and then devise solutions. They're from a really high concept sci-fi setting about a utopia with a population in the trillions run (without any real effort or trouble) by intellects so far beyond us that we can't even really comprehend how far beyond us they actually are. Exalts are not from a setting like that at all. I love the Culture books and I love Exalted, I don't think either gets much at all from a crossover, other than certain character concepts (Gurgeh would make for a really good Exalted character concept, but you'd have to take the bare bones core of the character and translate him across).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T17:21:54-07:00","id":481733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481710#post481710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Culture wouldn't lose any physical conflict because the Culture just effectors any Solar it recognises as a threat into non-existence within a fragment of a fragment of a nanosecond.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're acting like this matters in a setting where heroic willpower trumps the reality warping psychic powers of the authors of the universe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481710#post481710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Culture can't really lose any mental conflict because Exalt Charm capability tends to result in them being able to throw so many dice at a problem that it becomes trivial, which is how a Culture Mind operates too, except Culture Minds are lots of orders of magnitude more intelligent than any organic can possibly really be.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're acting like transcendent intelligence is somehow nonexistent in Exalted, when this is in fact the name of the day when it comes to any Exalt with an Intelligence/Lore excellency, to say nothing of their more conventional charms. The fall of the First Age was pretty much entirely brought about because no one could replicate or understand the greater works of the Solars. If that's not transcendent intelligence, then nothing is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481710#post481710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Culture doesn't really need to worry about Culture Humans becoming Exalts and fucking everything up because Culture Humans are notably balanced and psychologically stable individuals, raised as they are in what is a no shit actual utopia. Culture Humans wouldn't Exalt and suddenly decide that they are going to become conquering God-Kings, that fact that they have no interest in hierarchies of that kind and such is why they are the Culture.</div> </div> </div> </div>This sounds like an incredibly boring setting. You're right, no Culture human would Exalt because no GM would run a game there. There doesn't seem to be anything worthwhile to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481710#post481710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But the whole &quot;who would win in a fight&quot; argument is silly. I think it is fair to say that any kind of conventional problem is going to be easily solved by a Culture Mind, and if you're talking about Minds being unable to deal with out of context problems, they totally can do that. They'd adapt their understanding of how things work very quickly, and then devise solutions. They're from a really high concept sci-fi setting about a utopia with a population in the trillions run (without any real effort or trouble) by intellects so far beyond us that we can't even really comprehend how far beyond us they actually are.</div> </div> </div> </div>You basically just described The Heaven's Reach Shard. Now replace &quot;Minds&quot; with &quot;Lunar God-Kings with transcendent Intelligence Charms&quot; and &quot;Sidereal Spiritual Technicians with causality manipulating king-fu&quot;. This is excluding the Solars and the DBs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481710#post481710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exalts are not from a setting like that at all. I love the Culture books and I love Exalted, I don't think either gets much at all from a crossover, other than certain character concepts (Gurgeh would make for a really good Exalted character concept, but you'd have to take the bare bones core of the character and translate him across).</div> </div> </div> </div>The only crossovers that would really be in any way meaningful would be either Heaven's Reach or Gunstar. In the former they would be a novelty that might be allowed to continue existing under their own power so long as they don't run afoul the real heavies of the setting (His Devine Lunar Presence, the Stellar Intelligences, etc). In Gunstar however, the Minds would be ripped apart by SWLIHN or have already sworn fealty to Autochthon.<br /> <br /> Look, I'll admit that my overall knowledge of the Culture isn't up to what you apparently know, but I'm not really seeing anything here that is somehow magically beyond the Exalted. This goes double when you remember that Exalted operates under the rules of heroic mythic fantasy instead of real life. Joe Schmoe with a sword can parry a lightning bolt if he's quick enough and wants it bad enough. Trying to use real life physics and sci fi logic to say The Culture is somehow better than Exalted wont really work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2034,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","user":"JayTee","date":". 04-15-2015, 08:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-15T18:06:45-07:00","id":481794,"title":"","content":"I'll answer the &quot;Who would win - Exalts or Minds?&quot; question that way I answer all &quot;Who would win in a fight?&quot; questions - the winner is whoever you want. Just pick and choose setting assumptions until the side you favor is the logical victor.<br /> <br /> The question isn't really relevant to the topic, though - the OP asked &quot;Could the Culture emerge in Creation and/or Autocthonia?&quot; My answer is &quot;No, because the social context and potential resources available in Creation aren't anything like those in the pseudo-real Milky Way that the Culture novels are set in.&quot; Creation has or can make things that are Mind-like, and post scarcity is theoretically possible with incredible abuses of magic, but even if you managed both of those things you'd still live in a world where extreme power disparities between individual people make the formation of an egalitarian machine-managed anarchy unlikely.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T03:42:21-07:00","id":482186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481526#post481526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm getting the feeling that you'd rather pretend that Second Edition didn't happen at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>{sigh} I dislike statements like this. I feel as though they cast aspersions on me in a manner that I can't meaningfully challenge, resulting in something that I can't help but fixate on without being able to do anything with them in a constant frustrating loop. It's a splinter in my mind.<br /> <br /> I suppose the best I can manage is to state my intention in previous replies was to engage with the topic of this thread while prefacing it with a statement of my biases and preferences, hoping to add some intellectual discourse without just disparaging the premise.<br /> <br /> The only things I every try to pretend don't exist are posts like this so that I can move again.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T03:49:09-07:00","id":482189,"title":"","content":"I've been wondering if some of the premises of the First Age might make a &quot;post scarcity&quot; society impossible or undesirable.<br /> <br /> Like the idea that the patterned runes used to reinforce the world against the Wyld were based on commercial transactions and the movements of goods and property, and that things such as this might actually <i>require</i> scarcity, for a given meaning of the word.<br /> <br /> Now I've got an image of raksha who create environments of abundance and ease in order to make for stationary and indolent people who don't suitably ward against the more creeping subtlety of the Wyld. It's a bit like that warren with the snares in Watership Down.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T09:18:51-07:00","id":482448,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=481733#post481733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're acting like this matters in a setting where heroic willpower trumps the reality warping psychic powers of the authors of the universe.<br /> <br /> <br /> You're acting like transcendent intelligence is somehow nonexistent in Exalted, when this is in fact the name of the day when it comes to any Exalt with an Intelligence/Lore excellency, to say nothing of their more conventional charms. The fall of the First Age was pretty much entirely brought about because no one could replicate or understand the greater works of the Solars. If that's not transcendent intelligence, then nothing is.<br /> <br /> <br /> This sounds like an incredibly boring setting. You're right, no Culture human would Exalt because no GM would run a game there. There doesn't seem to be anything worthwhile to do.<br /> <br /> <br /> You basically just described The Heaven's Reach Shard. Now replace &amp;quot;Minds&amp;quot; with &amp;quot;Lunar God-Kings with transcendent Intelligence Charms&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;Sidereal Spiritual Technicians with causality manipulating king-fu&amp;quot;. This is excluding the Solars and the DBs.<br /> <br /> <br /> The only crossovers that would really be in any way meaningful would be either Heaven's Reach or Gunstar. In the former they would be a novelty that might be allowed to continue existing under their own power so long as they don't run afoul the real heavies of the setting (His Devine Lunar Presence, the Stellar Intelligences, etc). In Gunstar however, the Minds would be ripped apart by SWLIHN or have already sworn fealty to Autochthon.<br /> <br /> Look, I'll admit that my overall knowledge of the Culture isn't up to what you apparently know, but I'm not really seeing anything here that is somehow magically beyond the Exalted. This goes double when you remember that Exalted operates under the rules of heroic mythic fantasy instead of real life. Joe Schmoe with a sword can parry a lightning bolt if he's quick enough and wants it bad enough. Trying to use real life physics and sci fi logic to say The Culture is somehow better than Exalted wont really work.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, right.<br /> <br /> The culture is so far beyond anything in Creation that it is not funny. If a Solar can defeat a Mind in pretty much any contest the a Solar is far, far, far too powerful to exist in Creation, because Minds are smarter than anything in Creation to the point that they actively limit the simulations they run inside their own head because they realized that if they ran simulations to the fullest of their extent, the difference between killing people in real life and deleting them in the sim when it ends is basically nonexistent.<br /> <br /> I mean yeah, the culture is not a place where the culture stories are set, because it's a nice, safe, peaceful place filled with nice, safe, peaceful and well adjusted individuals. The stories are set in the places that the culture gently meddles in, and even then the stories are about the very, very rare occasions where things don't go exactly to plan, although even the the culture tends to win out.<br /> <br /> Creation is a place the culture messes around with because it feels a sense of responsibility towards the people who live there. Nobody, short of maybe (maybe) a primordial or something like that, is remotely a threat to the culture. I might as well try to fight the sun with a bow and arrow, it's just not going to happen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T09:36:36-07:00","id":482462,"title":"","content":"I mean we aren't talking about skynet here.<br /> <br /> We're talking about machines that sit skynet down and have a cup of tea and suggest that they might want to stop this genocide business because who really wants to get teleported casually into a nice safe hyperspatial box if they keep doing what they're doing?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T10:14:36-07:00","id":482501,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482448#post482448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I might as well try to fight the sun with a bow and arrow, it's just not going to happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that, in Exalted, that is a thing that can happen and a thing that can succeed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T10:21:34-07:00","id":482508,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482462#post482462\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean we aren't talking about skynet here.<br /> <br /> We're talking about machines that sit skynet down and have a cup of tea and suggest that they might want to stop this genocide business because who really wants to get teleported casually into a nice safe hyperspatial box if they keep doing what they're doing?</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like we're not reading the same books. In the ones I read, the Minds are fallible - specifically, they seem to be pretty bad at guessing what individual people are going to do and directing their behavior without being totally ham-fisted about it. The Culture always wins in the long term because of their ridiculous resource superiority, high tech, and what amounts to good psychohistorical predictive models, but the Minds tend to screw up when they have to deal with problems that are too small for the scale they were meant to deal with (e.g. Cheradenine Zakalwe), too big for the scale they were meant to deal with (e.g. &quot;The Excession&quot;), or that just come out of left field (e.g. that thing from the end of <u>Matter</u>).<br /> <br /> You also seem to be assuming that Exalts cannot defend themselves against high-tech methods of attack or build ridiculous superweapons of their own that could threaten the physical hardware the Minds are housed in, and I have no reason to think that is true.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 04-16-2015, 01:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T11:05:43-07:00","id":482588,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482448#post482448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The culture is so far beyond anything in Creation that it is not funny. If a Solar can defeat a Mind in pretty much any contest the a Solar is far, far, far too powerful to exist in Creation</div> </div> </div> </div>Solars adjust to fit the context they exist in and the enemies they're pitted against.<br /> <br /> That's why them having defeated the Primordials doesn't mean as much as some folks think it does.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T11:34:44-07:00","id":482606,"title":"","content":"If you wanted to cross over with Culture Minds, you *could* take the stance detailed here - <a href=\"http://exalted.xi.co.nz/wiki/Thus_Spake_Zargrabowski/HGDAndOrchDaiklaves\" target=\"_blank\">http://exalted.xi.co.nz/wiki/Thus_Sp...dOrchDaiklaves</a><br /> <br /> &quot;Look at it from the Exalted scale -- it's /just/ a nuclear reactor. Unless it was built out of the Magical Materials with epic feats and the sacrifices of thousands of lives, why would it be able to damage a weapon of legend like an orichalcum grand daiklave, which is a named and honored companion to a Celestial hero?&quot;<br /> <br /> In the same way, it's /just/ a Culture Mind, and the grand story of its being and nature rings hollow in a world of epic fantasy, so why should it be able to thwart the stratagems of a Celestial hero?<br /> <br /> But then you have to question why you're bothering including a Culture Mind in the first place. Their whole thing is being at a scale beyond... (except when that would lead to them being unrecognizable as generally affable, tolerant, sensible Left of Centre Westerners with varying personalities who are also some kind of AI supergods, or lead to boring stories.) It seems like having Exalts take down capital g God - either its actually God and that doesn't happen, or you don't take God on its own first principles and it's a sort of Worfing for whatever reason.<br /> <br /> It's all about genre, like Solar says.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T12:17:54-07:00","id":482616,"title":"","content":"This is a problem generally encountered with almost any Exalted crossover, and is why I'm not a fan of sticking Exalted into other things. One of the primary principles of the setting is &quot;the Exalted will always rise above any challenge that isn't purely moral or the Great Curse&quot;, and if you take that principle as default when you do the crossover, the answer is the Exalted win. They don't offer anything new to the other setting, they conquer it. It's more irritating than interesting, because it turns the other setting into little more than window dressing.<br /> <br /> But oftentimes what people mean when they think about an Exalted crossover is the idea of people endowed with sun/moon/fate/elemental-themed powers being introduced into the other setting. That idea has some grounds for at least some fanart, or a story about existing characters from the setting being endowed with those new powers. But that's not really crossing over with Exalted, it's crossing over with a thing that has outward similarities to Exalted. Rarely does such a crossover include any of the realpolitik or serious assessment of the fantasy tropes involved, when that's foundational to what makes Exalted Exalted rather than just a game about having superpowers..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/598-couch","name":"Couch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar598_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T12:47:26-07:00","id":482644,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482508#post482508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I feel like we're not reading the same books. In the ones I read, the Minds are fallible - specifically, they seem to be pretty bad at guessing what individual people are going to do and directing their behavior without being totally ham-fisted about it. The Culture always wins in the long term because of their ridiculous resource superiority, high tech, and what amounts to good psychohistorical predictive models, but the Minds tend to screw up when they have to deal with problems that are too small for the scale they were meant to deal with (e.g. Cheradenine Zakalwe), too big for the scale they were meant to deal with (e.g. &quot;The Excession&quot;), or that just come out of left field (e.g. that thing from the end of <u>Matter</u>).</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is a selection effect. The books are written about the <i>exceptionally rare</i> times that the Minds haven't neatly solved all the problems ahead of time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5376-atg","name":"AtG","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5376_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"691"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T13:00:06-07:00","id":482658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482588#post482588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's why them having defeated the Primordials doesn't mean as much as some folks think it does.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah I don't think this should ever be treated a trivial, or a done deal from the get-go, or anything other than an amazing upset by Humanity the Underdog.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T14:50:13-07:00","id":482832,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AtG</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482644#post482644\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think this is a selection effect. The books are written about the <i>exceptionally rare</i> times that the Minds haven't neatly solved all the problems ahead of time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. The Minds, 99.9999 (etc) times out of 100 solve problems they come across neatly and easily. I'm not saying that fighting them is like a Solar fighting the sun with a bow and arrow. I'm saying it's like me fighting the sun with a bow and arrow. How does a Solar even begin to try and fight a GSV? What could they possibly do to try and subvert it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T15:04:23-07:00","id":482837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482832#post482832\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exactly. The Minds, 99.9999 (etc) times out of 100 solve problems they come across neatly and easily. I'm not saying that fighting them is like a Solar fighting the sun with a bow and arrow. I'm saying it's like me fighting the sun with a bow and arrow. How does a Solar even begin to try and fight a GSV? What could they possibly do to try and subvert it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Assuming they're carrying a bow and arrow and they're Sufficiently Advanced in their magic and art? They shoot a lance of solar essence through the field armor, through the matter armor, and through the Mind's physical substrate itself.<br /> <br /> The nature of the Solar is that they have the privilege of being able to confront the ineffable with violence. The subtlety and deftness of the Mind outstrips them as the mind of a human genius outstrips a shark - but a shark may still tear the flesh from the body of a human and feast on the meat.<br /> <br /> The nature of the Solar is that there is nothing they cannot destroy, no boundary they cannot outstrip, no sanctitude beyond their desecration.<br /> <br /> How do they do this? It's magic! I don't gotta explain shit.<br /> <br /> That said, there are probably no Solars in the Age of Sorrows who come within a zillion miles of being able to do this. There's a big difference between, &quot;Hypothetically, it's not impossible for a Solar to become able to do this&quot; and &quot;I know a guy who can do that&quot;. But, given enough time to cultivate their power, and enough motivation to do so, they could become able.<br /> <br /> Edit: I should clarify, I'm not interested in gigantic pissing contests between the Culture and Creation, because hoo lawdy does the Culture win that one easily, but I felt it was worth stepping in to get poetic about the lack of Solar upper limits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":587,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","user":"Kukla","date":". 04-16-2015, 06:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T15:49:11-07:00","id":482888,"title":"","content":"Solars can't just do anything, though.<br /> <br /> They have their limits. That's kind of a fanon thing, that they can overcome any limit whatsoever. They can't. They are inordinately powerful, but they can't just do anything at all regardless of difficulty. The Culture is just... not the same as Creation. It's not in the same place at all. Could the Culture come from Creation? I guess, in the sense that the setting could transform entirely into something else, but one setting is a mythic swords and sorcery fantasy setting and the other is an ultra high concept pseudo hard sci-fi setting (not really hard, but hard in the sense that science is the hand-wave, not magic or psionics or whatever).<br /> <br /> Like, we're talking on the one hand about people who live on a single planetary sized mass, compared with a galaxy spanning society with trillions of people who all live on planetoid sized space ships which move through the boundaries of other universes and are piloted by entities who are of such a capacity that they invent entirely new sets of physics and explore what a universe based on those laws of physics would be like in intricate detail <i>for fun</i>. If a Solar beat up a Mind and made it cry, that would be dumb, if a Mind scolded a Solar for it's regressive and monarchist god-king desires, then that would be dumb as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-16T16:04:07-07:00","id":482890,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Couch</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482616#post482616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the primary principles of the setting is &quot;the Exalted will always rise above any challenge that isn't purely moral or the Great Curse&quot;\u2026</div> </div> </div> </div>It really isn't; either that, or is so watered-down as to be essentially meaningless.<br /> <br /> After all, the Dragon-Blooded of the Realm have consistently failed to conquer the River Province over centuries of warfare. The Lunars have <i>not</i> torn down the edifice of Dragon-Blooded hegemony and smashed the foundations, despite <i>millennia</i> spent trying. I'm sure Sidereals have been wanting to do away with Paradox since the Time Before Time, but they still get hit with Pattern Bite today. The Solars failed to turn back the tide of the Usurpation, and those that remained afterward failed to free the essences of their imprisoned fellows or rebuild their empire or take vengeance on their viziers and lieutenants. Even in the height of the First Age, no Solar mastered Void Circle Necromancy or breached the Seal of Eight Divinities or &quot;cured&quot; greed or hunger or anger or mortality or that most troublesome of problems, the human condition. <br /> <br /> I'd say that a much more poignant and relevant statement at the foundation of Exalted is that &quot;Some things are <i>really hard</i>, even if you have the power of a god.&quot;<br /> <br /> (With of course, Limpang-Tung's caveat. &quot;Natheless between Peg\u0101na and the Earth flutter ten thousand thousand prayers that beat their wings against the face of Death, and never for one of them hath the hand of the Striker been stayed, nor yet have tarried the feet of the Relentless One. Utter thy prayer! It may accomplish where failed ten thousand thousand.&quot;)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 04-16-2015, 07:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T03:54:22-07:00","id":483394,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482890#post482890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Lunars have <i>not</i> torn down the edifice of Dragon-Blooded hegemony and smashed the foundations, despite <i>millennia</i> spent trying.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've got an idea about how towards the end of the Shogunate, the finances and institutions were in such ruin because of factors including a lot of sabotage and attacks by Lunars that they were only a few decades away from a position in which travel and communications across the world would have been nigh impossible (making concentration of force pretty damn difficult) and enormous numbers of people had relocated to the &quot;wilderness&quot; in order to escape their debts or the plains in order to actually have some food, at which point Lunars would have been able to drift invisibly through the societies establishing lines of authority and ideas of lifestyle and picking off the isolated and impoverished Dragon Blooded one by one (albeit <i>very</i> quickly). After that, they could start openly working to take over the result and further shape it to their needs and liking.<br /> <br /> Then the Great Contagion came in and utterly destroyed all of their work; people at large became so diffuse and diverse and frequently falling in with the authorities of local gangsters like gods and stranger things, and the ruling hierarchy of the Dragon Blooded fell back on the Blessed Isle. So on the one hand, it became a target that was really kind of a thousand targets, which makes it hard to actually launch attacks with meaningful impact at any particular point, and on the other hand you have an enemy with the benefit of the interior lines (i.e. the smaller the area you have to defend, the easier it is to defend it).<br /> <br /> It's like the difference between the giant chihuaha and the hundred mini-t rexes; one of them has a lot more force, but it's slow and lumbering and has an obvious weakspot hanging down that impacts the whole body, and the other is numerous and fierce, weaker individually but with so many targets in so many configurations, where hitting one doesn't really effect any of the others.<br /> <br /> Which is ironic, because what the Age of Sorrows is to the Lunars is also what the Lunars are to the Realm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T04:02:54-07:00","id":483399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Couch</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> One of the primary principles of the setting is &quot;the Exalted will always rise above any challenge that isn't purely moral or the Great Curse&quot;</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> I wouldn't put it like that; more that there isn't really a challenge that is paradigmatically beyond them. The Exalted could have lost against the Primordials; apparently they could even have lost against the Niobrarans.<br /> <br /> Of course, that latter one introduces its own consideration; do these A.I.s have their own Exalted? I don't just mean known Exalted who are loyal to them, but distinct types of Exalted who Essentially express their principles? It might be fitting for them to. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=482888#post482888\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Solars can't just do anything, though.<br /> <br /> They have their limits. That's kind of a fanon thing, that they can overcome any limit whatsoever.</div> </div> </div> </div>In those terms it is. In terms where Solars have Charms to give them successes in a race proportional to the successes of their opponents?<br /> <br /> A Solar could outrun Mercury, although it's hardly a sure thing, and doesn't mean that she could never lose against anything that is not as fast as Mercury.<br /> <br /> Solars don't just beat anything, but if a Solar is up against a challenge in which the Solar has talents, the Solar is never out of the running before the contest even begins. Solars are designed to be capable of beating things more powerful than them, in and out-of-character. The Solar is the hero who defines mythos by their defiance of mythos. You can't design a scenario in which no Solar has a chance whatsoever, even if in the final tally the Solar in question loses.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T12:40:03-07:00","id":483802,"title":"","content":"This is kind of my point, though<br /> <br /> Exalts are based on those narrative assumptions (and they are narrative assumptions, because mechanically speaking, a Solar is as good as they have had XP spent on them), whereas the Culture is built on different narrative assumptions, those being that due to the Culture's technological and resource disparity it is nigh on literally impossible for anyone from a place like Creation to overcome, or even mildly concern. Exalted is based on the idea that a Solar can, potentially, put an arrow through a GSV, because that's awesome and heroic, whereas The Culture exists in a setting where this kind of idea is utterly laughable. Both of them need their settings to be the way they are, so both are essentially incompatible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 04-17-2015, 05:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T14:26:20-07:00","id":483891,"title":"","content":"Solar speaks wisely!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T14:33:13-07:00","id":483893,"title":"","content":"So we've established that Exalted and The Culture, as default settings, don't mesh very well.<br /> <br /> So how could a post-scarcity civilization arise in Creation? Is it even possible? What role might the Exalted play in it?<br /> <br /> If I ever get to play in a mixed Exalted game again, I would love to play as an Alchemical Exalted who comes from a post-scarcity civilization with the same values as The Culture because I think it'd be fun. Probably sent as a Contact Agent to do as they do in hierarchical societies (collapse them and bring them towards egalitarianism, however improbable that'd be in Exalted).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T14:39:02-07:00","id":483898,"title":"","content":"I'd say accomodating the premise of replicators would be a good place to start. I mean, even in the milieu of the setting as it is, something like extending the nature of Heaven to encompass the entire world, and a constant system of people praying for all of humanity...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T15:08:16-07:00","id":483912,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=483893#post483893\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So how could a post-scarcity civilization arise in Creation? Is it even possible?</div> </div> </div> </div>Create a device that processes Wyld energy into whatever material is required. Use it to strip mine infinity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T15:09:40-07:00","id":483913,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=483893#post483893\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So how could a post-scarcity civilization arise in Creation? Is it even possible? What role might the Exalted play in it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly this is an example of &quot;if it didn't happen in the First Age with the Exalted Host all working together, how would it be possible now?&quot; And they may have gotten close in the First Age with Wyld Shaping and the like, but they never really seemed to get close to the Federation in Star Trek where you can make food and essentials and ultimately ship components out of subatomic particles with the right technology. And if anything the Exalted would be required to maintain whatever process allowed for post-scarcity.<br /> <br /> The real problem there is how the Federation (and the Culture) intentionally oriented themselves toward developing and providing that quality of life to every person. It takes a unified political stance directed that way. In the absence of the unified vision, if one Exalt wanted to do that, I guarantee another would be trying to oppose them on principle or for their own ends etc etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T15:32:19-07:00","id":483945,"title":"","content":"So the consensus is still, &quot;Can't be done; Impossible; Don't bother&quot;?<br /> <br /> That's boring.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T17:09:51-07:00","id":484044,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=483945#post483945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So the consensus is still, &quot;Can't be done; Impossible; Don't bother&quot;?<br /> <br /> That's boring.</div> </div> </div> </div>My opinion is &quot;don't expect it to work out unless you neuter every other Exalt out there from fucking it up.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T17:36:25-07:00","id":484052,"title":"","content":"Not every premise fits every genre or setting. I would say that Exalted having sufficiently complex and developed ideas as to why a premise doesn't fit helps it be interesting about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T17:41:14-07:00","id":484056,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exalts are based on those narrative assumptions (and they are narrative assumptions, because mechanically speaking, a Solar is as good as they have had XP spent on them), whereas the Culture is built on different narrative assumptions, those being that due to the Culture's technological and resource disparity it is nigh on literally impossible for anyone from a place like Creation to overcome, or even mildly concern.</div> </div> </div> </div> I've got to admit a certain bias because, even disregarding any possible intersection with Exalted, I find the premise of this society as it has been articulated here to lack credibility. Which relates interestingly to Exalted in a different way, since a lot of what Exalted is based on is analysis of the kinds of trends that are <i>why</i> I think it lacks credibility. That and the contradiction between &quot;everybody gets to do what they want! So long as they're still constrained within these boundaries.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-17T21:53:35-07:00","id":484275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=483802#post483802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is kind of my point, though<br /> <br /> Exalts are based on those narrative assumptions (and they are narrative assumptions, because mechanically speaking, a Solar is as good as they have had XP spent on them), whereas the Culture is built on different narrative assumptions, those being that due to the Culture's technological and resource disparity it is nigh on literally impossible for anyone from a place like Creation to overcome, or even mildly concern. Exalted is based on the idea that a Solar can, potentially, put an arrow through a GSV, because that's awesome and heroic, whereas The Culture exists in a setting where this kind of idea is utterly laughable. Both of them need their settings to be the way they are, so both are essentially incompatible.</div> </div> </div> </div>Disagree. Like I said above, &quot;a place like Creation&quot; isn't something the Culture has any reference for.<br /> <br /> Basically, Solars are better at fighting things that are totally outside their normal frame of reference than the Culture is; and in the long run this means that the Culture is at least in plausibly threatened by them. A Culture Mind is not significantly more transcendently powerful than a Primordial; there's no particular reason why the charms that the Solars used to kill the (often bodiless) Primordials and make the Holy Tyrant feel fear wouldn't work on them. Something like &quot;I shape you out of existence on a molecular level&quot; is overwhelming and terrifying, but not something that they can't handle; whereas I think the Culture would have a lot more trouble dealing with people who can say &quot;no, I catch it and throw it back and force the Mind who sent it to take it as if they were a physical human, and they <i>die</i> even if that doesn't make sense.&quot;<br /> <br /> The narrative assumptions for the Solars are that they can and <i>have</i> killed things pretty much directly analogous to the transhuman AIs the Culture depends on; whereas I disagree with your assertion that the Culture's narrative assumptions make it impossible for them to be meaningfully threatened. (They can and have faced things that are entirely beyond them before; and I don't see any reason why Exaltation wouldn't fall into that category. They haven't lost <i>yet</i>, but I don't see anything in them that guarantees that they can't.)<br /> <br /> This doesn't mean that the Exalted will win, of course, it just means that I think that they have a chance in any reasonable interpretation. (And a lot depends on the Culture realizing that they're in danger before the Exalted develop defenses and attacks capable of taking out Culture Minds.)<br /> <br /> But saying &quot;the minds are transhuman intelligences, they can't lose to Solars!&quot; is silly. Intelligences that were in many ways more transcendent than them have said that and died; killing transcendent beings is literally what Solars are <i>for</i>. If the Culture reacts by thinking that way and just expects a few people to be able to handle the situation, they'll lose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 04-18-2015, 01:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T01:36:10-07:00","id":484375,"title":"","content":"Yeah but, as I said, the Solars live in a world where you can do that, the Culture live in a world where you can't. These are not compatible. <br /> <br /> If you cross them over, you have to pick what the world is like, what narrative assumptions you want to be looking at, and then depending on that, Solars are somehow able to cause the Culture trouble, or they are in fact laughable, because arrows cannot shoot down GSVs, even really powerful arrows, as you'd need to be doing billions of damage for that and having a power set defined as n + 1 (where n is the ability of you opponent to resist you) is ridiculous.<br /> <br /> As far as the Culture goes in terms of credibility, it's a very credible society, as credible as anything in Exalted anyway. It's a utopia because they figured out how you get rid of all the problems that stop a utopia forming, and everyone there is okay with that, though not everyone there is happy, because they're still people living lives. They do not want for anything they might actually desire, anyway. That makes sense, The fact that they interfere with other societies makes sense too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T02:27:27-07:00","id":484381,"title":"","content":"I have to disagree. This is not a case of genre conventions. I'm all for picking and choosing genre conventions! That's IMPORTANT!<br /> <br /> Certainly if the Culture's genre conventions are in effect, they are more likely to win than if Exalted's are.<br /> <br /> But 'Solars are able to take down battlecruisers with arrows and kill the unkillable' is not a genre convention. It is their capabilities. You can't have Solars unable to do that and still have Solars at all; you are not actually doing the crossover, then, you are just having the Culture find some dudes who happen to glow gold and are an inferior ripoff of the Solar Exalted. Solars are terrifying opponents, and that is not part of the genre conventions, but flat-out what they are and how they do things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2617-colapso","name":"Colapso","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2617_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T03:40:37-07:00","id":484393,"title":"","content":"For fun Culture vs Solar as we know it in combat. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> A culture warship is ready for battles playing out on a timescale not percievable for humans, aka it probably can deploy all its weaponry in the first tick or possibly before initiative is resolved.<br /> <br /> The warship is able to inflict a wide range of undodgable, unblockable, crippling, sickness and toxic effects on a scale from molecular to planet wide, most of these are faster then light and could in exalted only be resolved as reflexive attacks.<br /> <br /> The warship is able to keep doing so for nearly any length of time, including customizing and adding many (depending on size well up to millions) additional combatants (drones/missiles/smaller ships) to the battle.<br /> <br /> So what exalt can survive a endless amount of reflexive wide area attacks dealing instant death and keyword damage while unable to retaliate without charms due to mere range. Sounds like you need to get into action length perfect territory for it to be the least bit concievable and that's always been bullshit territory or Elder Exalt. Only other solution I see is an infinite range, infinite damage counterattack which very well could be possible <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> but not exactly common amongst even high Essence solars.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3085-chance","name":"chance","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T05:39:30-07:00","id":484423,"title":"","content":"There's many things that *could* happen; a Culture Mind could gain instant transcendent enlightenment into the nature of Essence and re-engineer itself perfectly to fit into Exalted's spiritual chi based cosmology, the same way it could rediscover all of hitherto existing human science in a microsecond based on data from its sensor instruments. There's no particular reason why not. They may also generally be geeky enough to have included infinitesimally rapid yet vastly detailed flickering daydreams of &quot;What would I do if transported to a magical universe?&quot; in their nigh infinite databanks.<br /> <br /> I guess the question is more like whether it would make good fanfic. Like Solar is saying, The Culture is (for better or worse) based on the idea that people who have (at best) these Dune style Imperial Space Monarchy societies are unenlightened and archaic and self defeating, and that societies with functionally no form of coercion except to prevent coercion and cruelty are possible, with enough science and resources, so lets talk about more interesting dilemmas that still exist once we accept this proposition. Those aren't the same pulp and epic fantasy assumptions Creation runs on!<br /> <br /> Asking what the Solars would do confronted with The Culture is almost like asking what they would do if they were transported to the Discworld or to the world of Monty Python and the Holy Grail or the Hitchhikers Guide to The Galaxy or even Dr Who*. They sort of don't belong in works which are on some level gently mocking towards the idea of epic heroism.<br /> <br /> Like, for an example, look at Elric at The End of Time by Moorcock, which mashes together both Moorcock's most famous doomed and romantic pulp fantasy hero and Dancers At The End of Time, his version of a decadent post scarcity supertech utopia that reacts with mildly bemusement at the primitive practices of the 20th century (and all earlier cultures) and their militarism. Elric becomes a bit of a pathetic and laughable figure, convinced that the Dancers are the Lords of Chaos and bemoaning his tragic fate to the Dancers' widespread befuddlement. At the same time, the Dancers themselves become comically taken with his charisma and style and somewhat inept at their attempts to use their godlike powers to recreate his genre within theirs (and it is IIRC quite funny). I'm not sure what else you could really have done with that situation. And the Culture shares quite a bit of Dancers At The End of Time's DNA (Dancers differs in a number of ways though - it's really more Dying Earth and a Comedy of Manners and not Space Opera at all).<br /> <br /> *or any number of post World War II British genre writers who in face of the of that conflict and particular traditions of philosophy and humour, chose to satirise both power and warrior heroism while making the case for the resilience of humanism, decency, common sense, and absurdity in the face of the inhuman scale conflicts of fantasy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T07:02:31-07:00","id":484517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>chance</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=484393#post484393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For fun Culture vs Solar as we know it in combat. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> A culture warship is ready for battles playing out on a timescale not percievable for humans, aka it probably can deploy all its weaponry in the first tick or possibly before initiative is resolved.<br /> <br /> The warship is able to inflict a wide range of undodgable, unblockable, crippling, sickness and toxic effects on a scale from molecular to planet wide, most of these are faster then light and could in exalted only be resolved as reflexive attacks.<br /> <br /> The warship is able to keep doing so for nearly any length of time, including customizing and adding many (depending on size well up to millions) additional combatants (drones/missiles/smaller ships) to the battle.<br /> <br /> So what exalt can survive a endless amount of reflexive wide area attacks dealing instant death and keyword damage while unable to retaliate without charms due to mere range. Sounds like you need to get into action length perfect territory for it to be the least bit concievable and that's always been bullshit territory or Elder Exalt. Only other solution I see is an infinite range, infinite damage counterattack which very well could be possible <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> but not exactly common amongst even high Essence solars.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well your basic surprise negator + perfect defense paranoia combo will let them survive. I don't know Solars well enough to know what charms would allow them to fight back, probably something in the E6+ range? They would certainly exist though.<br /> <br /> However we're also forgetting that the Solars aren't the only players in the game. When you're throwing around that much fire power it would make sense for the Unconquered Sun to intervene and unlike with the Primordials there'd be no oaths protecting the Minds. <br /> <br /> Now, if it's not regular Creation it'd depend on the setting. With Gunstar Autochonthia setting there's enough elder Exalts, the Exalts are unified, and they have enough tech that they'd be able to fight a mind on equal footing. In Heaven's Reach they have fought and won against things like the Minds but they're so dis-unified at the time that I'd give the advantage to the Minds. I don't see the Minds having any problem with the Burn Legend setting, and for the Modern Age.... Mmm mmm.... I'd give the advantage to the Minds, but between the experience and infrastructure of the Infernals, plus their access to third circle demons, it wouldn't be a sure thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5257-queshire","name":"Queshire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5257_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T07:06:04-07:00","id":484518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Colapso</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=484381#post484381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have to disagree. This is not a case of genre conventions. I'm all for picking and choosing genre conventions! That's IMPORTANT!<br /> <br /> Certainly if the Culture's genre conventions are in effect, they are more likely to win than if Exalted's are.<br /> <br /> But 'Solars are able to take down battlecruisers with arrows and kill the unkillable' is not a genre convention. It is their capabilities. You can't have Solars unable to do that and still have Solars at all; you are not actually doing the crossover, then, you are just having the Culture find some dudes who happen to glow gold and are an inferior ripoff of the Solar Exalted. Solars are terrifying opponents, and that is not part of the genre conventions, but flat-out what they are and how they do things.</div> </div> </div> </div>But Solars being able to potentially do anything does not and never has been actually the case, and even if it was, the vast majority of Solars do face limitations of everything they can do. While theoretically yes a Solar could shoot an arrow which breaks the laws of physics so hard that it kills a GSV, that's actually never going to be the case in a game, where the amount of dice you can roll and successes you can get is limited to how good you happen to be at a thing.<br /> <br /> Genre incompatability is a thing. Exalts are magic, and magic isn't real in Culture-land, if it was, they'd have mastered it. Compare the two and you have to decide who is right, are Exalts awesome heroes who can indeed overcome any boundary and because this new boundary has presented itself, they will, or are the laws of physics rather more inviolate than that, sorry, each cell in your body was just given it's own private square foot of land within the heart of a nearby star. This is not hyperbole, btw. This is what the Culture can actually do, with their tech. Not that they would do anything so flashy and glorified, especially if it was in the act of killing someone (if you are going to kill someone at least have the good grace to do it quickly and efficiently and without trying to look impressive).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T07:21:34-07:00","id":484532,"title":"","content":"I don't believe that the Culture has a game so I don't see what winds up happening at the game board has to do with this. Far more important is talking about narrative things since both actually have these, and while Genre Incompatibility might be a thing, it's not one that does here. I refer you to the Stellar Intelligences of the Heaven's Reach shard in Shards of the Exalted Dream.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5257-queshire","name":"Queshire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5257_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"153"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T21:37:08-07:00","id":485070,"title":"","content":"The concept that the Culture's genre conventions won't allow a Solar to kill a GSV is ridiculous. In the Culture universe GSV's have been destroyed in conflicts with other powers. Thus fundamentally, a Solar is capable of destroying one as well.<br /> <br /> Now, this of course requires the hypothetical Solar to be a crazy powerful elder Exalt, but I believe we've seen enough charms to make a combo that could in fact do the task. The Solar could even GET it destroying all backup copies of the onboard Mind killing it permanently.<br /> <br /> What this is failing to point out is that while the Solar was activating the combo, said Mind was scanning him, gaining a fundamental understanding of Essence channeling, successfully calculating the Solar's Essence pool and the cost of the combo, as then transmitting all said data back to the other Minds of the Culture. And if the Minds decide to go for a round 2, it will be with an entire fleet calculated to be large enough to mote tap him.<br /> <br /> The fact that a Solar could kill a GSV has no real baring outside of that single tactical battle, and in no way undermines the Culture or its genre.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, what would determine the result of a long term conflict would be determining the actual reason of said conflict, as well as the size of the force on the Solar side of the conflict. The Culture has a overwhelming numerical advantage in numbers compared to basically every form of Exalted we've seen. But at the same time, the Culture has an overwhelming desire to not actually harm the Exalted Host if at all possible.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Do not meddle in the affairs of dragons, for you are crunchy and taste good with ketchup.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/608-yoshar","name":"Yoshar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar608_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T22:00:11-07:00","id":485080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yoshar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=485070#post485070\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What this is failing to point out is that while the Solar was activating the combo, said Mind was scanning him, gaining a fundamental understanding of Essence channeling, successfully calculating the Solar's Essence pool and the cost of the combo, as then transmitting all said data back to the other Minds of the Culture. And if the Minds decide to go for a round 2, it will be with an entire fleet calculated to be large enough to mote tap him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Three very important points of order:<br /> <br /> - Native Charms, mote pools and essence scores do not exist in-setting, The Exalted do these things because they are Exalted, not because they spend MP to cast spells.<br /> <br /> - Essence is a supernatural phenomena and beyond scientific examination, a Mind would be incapable of examining it, or understanding it.<br /> <br /> - This assumes that an Exalt would attempt a head on attack, rather than simply hacking their systems and introducing a virus to destroy a Mind from within. No matter how sophisticated, Minds are a mortal construction, no mortal machine can stand against a supernaturally empowered hacking attempt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-18T23:41:01-07:00","id":485185,"title":"","content":"I think, at the end of the day, the Exalted were created to do the impossible, break the unbreakable (row row fight the power), and I think that's something worth keeping in mind when discussing crossovers. <br /> <br /> Of course, the big problem is, a Solar with zero dots in combat abilities and an elder with a full array of Essence 10 combat charms... there's a huge, huge difference between their capabilities. So whenever it comes down to, &quot;Exalt vs whatever&quot; we should probably define what sort of Exalt we're looking at and what sort of power he has because otherwise people will just be arguing past one another, since everyone has a very different idea of what a &quot;generic Exalt&quot; looks like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-19T01:38:58-07:00","id":485220,"title":"","content":"I was thinking about considerations for what makes for a &quot;wealthy&quot; peasant, or even a well-payed one (since one of the points of post-Black Death Europe was an across the board increase in wages and lowering of prices), and it got me thinking that depending on what one's definition of &quot;post scarcity&quot; is, the First Age as it has always generally been hinted or portrayed as being would qualify. If it means &quot;there is an abundance of raw materials and such an efficient means of processing and transporting them, as well as a sufficient variety of ancillary services that lots of people can have jobs and a general social ideology that people deserve to have the means to determine their own lifestyle and generally have a decent quality of life&quot;, then I would say that at its highest points the Realm would have qualified. I don't think it would have been generally difficult for a network of interdependent societies to which the Exalted institutionally contribute to have been such that no matter what your lifestyle or occupation, you could have had a spacious living area filled with a variety of manufactured goods and an abundance of food, and could spend nights or days visiting and contributing to centres of culture or entertainment, and even if there weren't really jobs for you it wasn't difficult to go out and reap food from the land and live in communities where all of those kinds of things are kind of shared communally. I'm not saying it's trivial, just that I think Exalted would be really good at harnessing the limited resources of the Earth (and manipulating the essential substance and the gods who oversee it) to create such abundance that, combined with the reduced need to harness massive amounts of human labour in tedious and dangerous tasks, people didn't really need to live in poverty as we would recognise it. I'd see the major logistical concerns of their society as maintaining all of the cultural expressions and sacrifices and geomancy necessary to power the magics that would make such abundance and processing and transport possible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-19T21:24:50-07:00","id":486015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yoshar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=485070#post485070\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What this is failing to point out is that while the Solar was activating the combo, said Mind was scanning him, gaining a fundamental understanding of Essence channeling, successfully calculating the Solar's Essence pool and the cost of the combo, as then transmitting all said data back to the other Minds of the Culture. And if the Minds decide to go for a round 2, it will be with an entire fleet calculated to be large enough to mote tap him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because, for some reason, said Solar didn't use Stealth Charms to avoid detection and subsequent scanning as it ganked said hypothetical mind.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T04:09:37-07:00","id":486209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yoshar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=485070#post485070\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And if the Minds decide to go for a round 2, it will be with an entire fleet calculated to be large enough to mote tap him.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I really hope Third Edition will include a decent way to capture the essence of &quot;fight somebody to exhaustion&quot; without it amounting to &quot;the timer on all of your buffs ran out, and now you're nothing more than a hairless ape&quot;.<br /> <br /> I mean, acknowledging that between the way mote regeneration and Charm costs work in the new Edition, mote tap on its own won't really be a thing anymore.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T04:22:46-07:00","id":486218,"title":"","content":"Well, maybe the [momentum] system can model it to some extent. Fight someone until they run out of momentum perhaps, representing that they're exhausted, and then you can take the killing blow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T05:05:26-07:00","id":486234,"title":"","content":"You could probably model physical exhaustion through the initiative system, especially if you expanded the combat time. So normally one action takes a few seconds, but now one action represents a minute or even an hour, and you simply assume that your and your opponent have been engaged in a furious, equal struggle, and these actions represent unique moments where you can get a major advantage over them. <br /> <br /> In such a situation it might be reasonable to require rolling stamina + resistance, and as you fail it drains your initiative, or otherwise inflicts penalties to the point where you're too tired to fight back (I did something like this back in First Edition to model an epic battle which was supposed to take place from sunrise to sunset).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T05:30:01-07:00","id":486262,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I think that works well. I've done that before once or twice with mote pools (ie said &quot;make opposed stamina+resistance rolls&quot; with each roll being an hour, and the loser of the roll no longer has any motes. 2nd ed combats were so long anyway, that frankly it sped things up a lot. Especially when the two high soak, regenerating Lunar PCs decided to fight each other).<br /> Day-long fights are something that's very in-genre for Exalted, but doesn't actually work with the rules (of both Exalted and pretty much every other RPG's combat system, since in most systems fights normally take less than a minute. In high-level 3rd ed DnD fights used to last about 12 seconds).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T08:51:17-07:00","id":486437,"title":"","content":"Mildly off topic, but I've always wondered what the balance implications would be of a house rule that said Exalts cannot fall below 5-10 motes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T09:43:10-07:00","id":486477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=486437#post486437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mildly off topic, but I've always wondered what the balance implications would be of a house rule that said Exalts cannot fall below 5-10 motes.</div> </div> </div> </div>You mean giving them functionally infinite motes, but a point beyond which their ability to activate high cost Charms is restricted?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T10:19:19-07:00","id":486489,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyeudo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=486477#post486477\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You mean giving them functionally infinite motes, but a point beyond which their ability to activate high cost Charms is restricted?</div> </div> </div> </div>Kind of. I emphatically reject the notion that mote tapping is a thing that happens in-setting. An Exalt should always be able to draw upon some part of his powers, even when utterly exhausted. The rule is intended to give an Exalt the ability to always have access to his more fundamental charms while restricting access to his high power abilities if he's on the losing end of a fight.<br /> <br /> Thinking about it, 10 motes might be a bit much, but always being able to spend at least 5 motes on charms (combo a 2 and 3 mote charm together, or apply a 5 die excellency, or some other form of mote expenditure that totals to 5 motes) feels like it wouldn't break anything too badly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T10:25:32-07:00","id":486493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=486489#post486489\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thinking about it, 10 motes might be a bit much, but always being able to spend at least 5 motes on charms (combo a 2 and 3 mote charm together, or apply a 5 die excellency, or some other form of mote expenditure that totals to 5 motes) feels like it wouldn't break anything too badly.</div> </div> </div> </div>Post 2.5? You can probably make it work. I'm sure someone more familiar with 2.5 could find an exploit somewhere, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T10:34:38-07:00","id":486500,"title":"","content":"We know in third edition Exalted regain 5 motes every round. So functionally they always start each round with 5 motes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T10:43:01-07:00","id":486503,"title":"","content":"Yeah, and that makes me happy. My house rule would go a little further than that, but I can accept this compromise between what 2.5e gave me and what I wanted.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T11:52:13-07:00","id":486579,"title":"","content":"On a couple of your points from upthread JayTee (although probably no one wants to restart the main thread!)<br /> <br /> Assuming any translation of the Minds to a Creation that is anything like the Minds as they are in The Culture (for all that I've said this probably wouldn't be a story I'd think it serves Creation or The Culture well to try and tell) :<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=485080#post485080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- Essence is a supernatural phenomena and beyond scientific examination, a Mind would be incapable of examining it, or understanding it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing with Minds is, they're not like Commander Data or HAL9000. Sympathetic or unsympathetic but regardless cold or strange machines of logic and science who are, because of this, somehow missing an essential facet of human nature. They're morally heroic and deeply compassionate. They have watched c-beams glitter in the dark near the Tannhauser Gate. So to speak. They could create models of *people* where the map is the territory (did they not judge it morally wrong to do so).<br /> <br /> To the extent spirituality exists in the universe they are in, they will grok it. And understanding of essence is, if we are putting a label on it, spiritual knowledge. And habitually Minds are many orders of magnitude faster thinkers and more knowledgeable than any man can be.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=485080#post485080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">- This assumes that an Exalt would attempt a head on attack, rather than simply hacking their systems and introducing a virus to destroy a Mind from within. No matter how sophisticated, Minds are a mortal construction, no mortal machine can stand against a supernaturally empowered hacking attempt.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a lot like saying, &quot;No matter how vast the ocean, if it is only material and Exalt can't fail to swim it.&quot;. Nice in theory, but how vast does the ocean get before it doesn't hold true? The Minds are that mortal ocean that is practically infinite in extent.<br /> <br /> Although I doubt the Minds would actually get in fights with the Exalted.<br /> <br /> I imagine it would go more like this:<br /> <br /> Mortal: &quot;Strange visitor, there are these terrible sorcerous beings called the Anathema and...&quot;<br /> <br /> Mind (with a pithy Mind name): &quot;Woah, let me stop you right there. So you're saying that there are these guys called the Solar Exalted, and this obviously fake religion was made up to demonise them, and that's was done because they labour under this Great Curse that was placed against them for the defeat of these ancient &quot;Primordials&quot;... <br /> <br /> (What? Oh yes, sorry, I figured that all out from listening to the observing every mortal on your.... &quot;planet&quot;, reading through all your libraries and simulating your history from the data while you spoke.)<br /> <br /> Now as for these Solars, I have just compiled a list of probably beneficial spiritual practices from my database of the collected religion and philosophy of a 10,000 civilizations over 500,000 worlds, and since that stuff really seems to do something around here, I really think I have a crack at a good plan that could help get over this.<br /> <br /> (and btw, that Hell thing - very not cool! The Culture's not really down with Hell. We'll really have to do work something out there.)&quot;<br /> <br /> (Of course, that's part of why Exalted x The Culture would be a weird, perhaps unsatisfying story looked at from the themes of either one).<br /> <br /> (also realized this thread makes me want to read a Culture book again - thanks Wulf).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-20T13:59:56-07:00","id":486714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=486437#post486437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mildly off topic, but I've always wondered what the balance implications would be of a house rule that said Exalts cannot fall below 5-10 motes.</div> </div> </div> </div>in 2.0 it means Solaroids can functionally never lose. It's substantially better in 2.5, but like...why would you even do this? Your actions effectively have no consequences if you can't run yourself out of resources and have an infinite safety net of bad 2e mechanics to draw from to save yourself. If you burn yourself out of resources because you're not being smart,<i> you deserve what you get.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T13:53:57-07:00","id":487592,"title":"","content":"Ghosthead does a great job of summarizing why it would be profoundly difficult to make a satisfying crossover between Creation and Culture Space.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T16:03:18-07:00","id":487760,"title":"","content":"You know, I don't really use the term &quot;Mary Sue&quot;... ever. It doesn't even really exist in my orbit of critical terminology. But these people...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T16:22:45-07:00","id":487772,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=487760#post487760\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know, I don't really use the term &quot;Mary Sue&quot;... ever. It doesn't even really exist in my orbit of critical terminology. But these people...</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the Culture is a Mary Sue society. Certainly, I perceive it is far from flawless, even if its own people think otherwise... but then again, I don't have as positive a view of the Culture in general or the Minds in particular as I have seen in this thread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 04-21-2015, 07:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T16:54:12-07:00","id":487797,"title":"","content":"It was more about that whole &quot;Imma figure out everything instantly and comprehensively cause I'm just so awesome, yo&quot;. I can just see a big computer screen with a cutesy smug grinning face just begging to have somebody punch it (which it would just respond to by appearing on a different screen and condescendingly talking about how pointlessly mean you are and maybe you'd like a list of aggression exercises).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T17:01:06-07:00","id":487801,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=487797#post487797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was more about that whole &quot;Imma figure out everything instantly and comprehensively cause I'm just so awesome, yo&quot;. I can just see a big computer screen with a cutesy smug grinning face just begging to have somebody punch it (which it would just respond to by appearing on a different screen and condescendingly talking about how pointlessly mean you are and maybe you'd like a list of aggression exercises).</div> </div> </div> </div>It's really not as hyperbolic as your portrayal.<br /> <br /> It's a civilization that prizes certain values and actually has the power and resources to make those values manifest. It's a civilization essentially run by hyper-advanced machines who are constantly upgrading themselves and feel as if they have a moral calling to make the universe a better place (as they see it) and try to pay it forward given the resources at their disposal, and some of the organic people who live in that civilization feel the same way. And yes some of that involves meddling, but what hyper-powerful culture wouldn't meddle? Would you call a First Age Solar so Sue-ish if they did that with their realm, and locked it away so nobody could really mess with it?<br /> <br /> Really though Minds are generally setting background in the Culture novels and the real stories revolve around organics who are dealing ins one way with the Culture, or are the Culture's Contact/Special Circumstances agents out in the universe meddling.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T17:08:57-07:00","id":487803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=487801#post487801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really though Minds are generally setting background in the Culture novels and the real stories revolve around organics who are dealing ins one way with the Culture, or are the Culture's Contact/Special Circumstances agents out in the universe meddling.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, that's mildly more interesting, then. Based on what people were saying about the Minds in this thread, I was becoming convinced that the culture was the most boring, worthless and uninteresting setting ever, because what was the point of anything even remotely resembling conflict, tension or drama happening if the Minds could solve it all with their super special snowflake intelligence powers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T17:27:18-07:00","id":487808,"title":"","content":"I know people who won't give either Exalted or The Culture novels the time of day because they've encountered them in this kind of &quot;my dad can beat up your dad&quot; style online debate and can't see how anyone derives plot or enjoyment from them.<br /> <br /> It's frankly unfair to both.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-21T17:44:29-07:00","id":487816,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=487803#post487803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, that's mildly more interesting, then. Based on what people were saying about the Minds in this thread, I was becoming convinced that the culture was the most boring, worthless and uninteresting setting ever, because what was the point of anything even remotely resembling conflict, tension or drama happening if the Minds could solve it all with their super special snowflake intelligence powers.</div> </div> </div> </div>You'll be happy to learn, then, that just about every book covers a situation that at least one Mind was either not prepared for or needed the assistance of something human-sized to deal with. They're not omnipotent.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T08:18:04-07:00","id":488283,"title":"","content":"Far from it. They get wrecked on the regular.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/587-kukla","name":"Kukla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"183"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T09:12:54-07:00","id":488362,"title":"","content":"Well no, they don't. They very rarely even get vexed. But the books are about when they do.<br /> <br /> And really, describing the core setting of the culture books from an objective non narrative perspective doesn't do them justice at all. They're really, really good. Iain M Banks was considered one of the greatest living science fiction writers, if not the greatest, before he passed away, and for good reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 04-22-2015, 12:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T09:20:10-07:00","id":488366,"title":"","content":"All comparisons between the settings aside: The protagonist of Use Of Weapons makes a <i>spectacular</i> Dawn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/513-gatts","name":"Gatts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar513_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T13:22:18-07:00","id":488630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=487803#post487803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I was becoming convinced that the culture was the most boring, worthless and uninteresting setting ever, because what was the point of anything even remotely resembling conflict, tension or drama happening if the Minds could solve it all with their super special snowflake intelligence powers.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sound a bit like playing in the 2e canonical First Age :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T14:15:41-07:00","id":488703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's really not as hyperbolic as your portrayal.</div> </div> </div> </div> Then people should maybe leave out cute little explanations of how they would instantly figure out everything wrong with the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T15:47:21-07:00","id":488801,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488703#post488703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Then people should maybe leave out cute little explanations of how they would instantly figure out everything wrong with the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>But where's the fun in that? Next your going to ask people to characterize the Games of Divinity as something more numinous than xbox or crack, Autochthon as something besides Primus, and Exaltations as miracles instead of autonomous hunter-killer drones.<br /> <br /> What would we have to talk about then?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1144-exthalion","name":"Exthalion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1144_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T16:21:37-07:00","id":488814,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Exthalion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488801#post488801\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What would we have to talk about then?</div> </div> </div> </div>There's always Lunars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T19:16:40-07:00","id":488958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488703#post488703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Then people should maybe leave out cute little explanations of how they would instantly figure out everything wrong with the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>They kind of would? But, at the same time, if a book was written about the Culture meddling with Creation, then that would mean that a wrench had got caught in the Culture's machinery somewhere and so things would not be that simple. Most of the time, the Minds get stuff sorted, job jobbed, no problems. On rare occasions, though, we get a Culture novel.<br /> <br /> I dunno. You've not read the books so it feels like you'll not really get a good idea of how the Culture works as a narrative tool because it's something best explained by the books. They're really good, you see.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T20:12:24-07:00","id":488975,"title":"","content":"Reaaaally not doing yourself any favors by masturbating over how super special snowflake the culture is. I currently remain unconvinced that it's anything worth paying attention to, and claims of transcendent intelligence beating everything are dubious at best, especially when the Exalted themselves have easy access to transcendent intelligence via Excellences.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zelbinnean</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488630#post488630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sound a bit like playing in the 2e canonical First Age :P</div> </div> </div> </div>The main argument I have against this is that at least in the First Age conflict could come from other Exalts who had an agenda that was different than, or simply opposite to, your own. <br /> <br /> Is there anything like this in the series? Some kind of anti-mind who works to subvert the culture minds so that their own plans go awry, necessitating the use of non-mind agents? 'cause otherwise I don't see how their super special snowflake intelligence powers wouldn't have solved every problem in the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T20:39:30-07:00","id":488986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488975#post488975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The main argument I have against this is that at least in the First Age conflict could come from other Exalts who had an agenda that was different than, or simply opposite to, your own.<br /> <br /> Is there anything like this in the series? Some kind of anti-mind who works to subvert the culture minds so that their own plans go awry, necessitating the use of non-mind agents? 'cause otherwise I don't see how their super special snowflake intelligence powers wouldn't have solved every problem in the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, Minds have been known to work at cross-purposes - or even against themselves, under the right circumstances. Also, despite what Solar says, they're not the biggest fish in the pond - their setting is a big galaxy containing an indefinite number of civilizations, some of which are close to or even beyond the Culture in terms of development.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T21:29:17-07:00","id":489010,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488986#post488986\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure, Minds have been known to work at cross-purposes - or even against themselves, under the right circumstances. Also, despite what Solar says, they're not the biggest fish in the pond - their setting is a big galaxy containing an indefinite number of civilizations, some of which are close to or even beyond the Culture in terms of development.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's... better, I suppose, but it doesn't really address my current issue with the series. Lemme rephrase the question so I can get a better understanding: What is the main source of conflict in the culture series, and why have the minds not immediately solved it? Assuming that the minds are as intelligent as claimed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-22T22:22:45-07:00","id":489065,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489010#post489010\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's... better, I suppose, but it doesn't really address my current issue with the series. Lemme rephrase the question so I can get a better understanding: What is the main source of conflict in the culture series, and why have the minds not immediately solved it? Assuming that the minds are as intelligent as claimed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Looking it up it seems the primary thing setting conflict is the fact that The Culture has led to boredom in its society. The result of this is that they have moved onto the project of uplifting other societies. Which seem to indicate that the problems come from a sort of &quot;we know what to do, now we have the tricky part of doing it.&quot; I'm sure there's more to it and its more complex than that, but that seems to be the most basic one.<br /> <br /> Actually looking at that angle it doesn't seem too different from some parts of Exalted.I mean Holden once said that part of the challenge in Exalted isn't overthrowing the evil ruler its trying to actually make people's lives better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3400,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","user":"nalak42","date":". 04-23-2015, 01:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T01:39:05-07:00","id":489151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488986#post488986\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure, Minds have been known to work at cross-purposes - or even against themselves, under the right circumstances. Also, despite what Solar says, they're not the biggest fish in the pond - their setting is a big galaxy containing an indefinite number of civilizations, some of which are close to or even beyond the Culture in terms of development.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never said they were the biggest fish in the pond! I don't even think they're supposed to be the most advanced society in the Involved, they definitely aren't advanced as any Sublimed society (not that it really makes any difference, as the Sublimed barely interact with anything the Culture are interested in).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T01:39:51-07:00","id":489152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489010#post489010\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's... better, I suppose, but it doesn't really address my current issue with the series. Lemme rephrase the question so I can get a better understanding: What is the main source of conflict in the culture series, and why have the minds not immediately solved it? Assuming that the minds are as intelligent as claimed.</div> </div> </div> </div>The main source of narrative in the Culture books is that they are all set outside the Culture, in places that the Culture is meddling in to try and improve.<br /> <br /> And I'm not masturbating over how advanced the Culture are. They are that advanced. That is kind of the point of the Culture!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T02:12:03-07:00","id":489180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488958#post488958\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They kind of would? But, at the same time, if a book was written about the Culture meddling with Creation, then that would mean that a wrench had got caught in the Culture's machinery somewhere and so things would not be that simple.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is what I'm talking about when I say that the premises, as they've been presented here, strike me as internally contradictory.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T02:31:59-07:00","id":489190,"title":"","content":"Really you guys I do not think there is much point in my arguing with you about the merits of a setting used by a series of (critically acclaimed, highly successful and very influential) books because you've not read any of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T05:18:06-07:00","id":489331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489190#post489190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2026critically acclaimed, highly successful and very influential\u2026</div> </div> </div> </div><i>Avatar</i> was critically-acclaimed (who <i>didn't</i> say it was a beautiful movie?), highly successful (seeing how James Cameron made more money than Croesus), and was influential enough to usher in an age of 3D movies (and movies shittily converted to 3D in post) that still hasn't completely died off (though we all want it to). That doesn't mean it's not the cinematic equivalent of Applebee's.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 04-23-2015, 08:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T05:50:31-07:00","id":489399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489190#post489190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really you guys I do not think there is much point in my arguing with you about the merits of a setting used by a series of (critically acclaimed, highly successful and very influential) books because you've not read any of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the problem is so much with the merits of the books themselves as it is how you have presented them.<br /> <br /> Also, where do you get this idea from that nothing &quot;interesting&quot; happens in the Culture outside of what is written in the books (or could be in hypothetical books)? Is there no possibility that undocumented drama exists?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T06:11:24-07:00","id":489410,"title":"","content":"Sure I am willing to say that the books have not been presented as well here as the actual books present themselves, they're professional novels, there are posts on an internet forum.<br /> <br /> As far as nothing interesting happening in the Culture goes, loads of interesting stuff happens, but not in the sense that there's nefarious schemes and wars and stuff like that which you might get in Creation, it's all interpersonal relationship stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T06:17:18-07:00","id":489413,"title":"","content":"I mean, if none of you have actually read the books then you're best off just, you know, reading one or two of them.<br /> <br /> Until you do it's going to be very difficult having this discussion. We're comparing two things, but half of you only know about one of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T07:12:48-07:00","id":489437,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=488958#post488958\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They kind of would? But, at the same time, if a book was written about the Culture meddling with Creation, then that would mean that a wrench had got caught in the Culture's machinery somewhere and so things would not be that simple. Most of the time, the Minds get stuff sorted, job jobbed, no problems. On rare occasions, though, we get a Culture novel.<br /> <br /> I dunno. You've not read the books so it feels like you'll not really get a good idea of how the Culture works as a narrative tool because it's something best explained by the books. They're really good, you see.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here's your problem. You keep presenting the Culture as a Mary-Suetopia which can instantly solve all Creations problems and nobody in Creation stands a chance of opposing them, ever.<br /> <br /> You should approach it from the view that any crossover between Creation and Culture <i>will be</i> a <i>Culture Novel</i>, and accept that that means any Mind involved <i>will have to have </i>problems dealing with Creation, in order for an interesting story to be generated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T07:24:06-07:00","id":489441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489413#post489413\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, if none of you have actually read the books then you're best off just, you know, reading one or two of them.<br /> <br /> Until you do it's going to be very difficult having this discussion. We're comparing two things, but half of you only know about one of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>You do realize that the more you talk about the Culture, the less I want to read about Mary-Suetopia, right?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1520732-forged-by-purpose-alchemicals-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Forged By Purpose: The Alchemicals</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1413451-dead-but-not-gone-ghosts\" target=\"_blank\">Dead But Not Gone: Ghosts</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/228291-ghosts-a-revision-peach\" target=\"_blank\">Ghosts: A Revision</a> (2nd Edition)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/316-masters-of-the-industrial-elements-2e-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Masters of the Industrial Elements</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1157362-from-the-crucible-crafting-as-a-struggle-peach\" target=\"_blank\">\u200bFrom The Crucible: Crafting As A Struggle</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/74-kyeudo","name":"Kyeudo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar74_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1723"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T07:49:22-07:00","id":489471,"title":"","content":"Well, that's up to you. Read it or don't, not my concern.<br /> <br /> But I am just saying that if you haven't read any Culture books then you can hardly weigh in on a discussion on the Culture with regards to Exalted and expect me to really take your views on the Culture seriously.<br /> <br /> They aren't &quot;Mary Sues&quot; (God if I could kill one phrase from reality forever...), they're a really advanced society who have used their incredible technology to effectively solve the vast majority of problems we face today as a species and are run by stupendously intelligent machine Minds, this is kind of the point of the Culture. They don't generate the traditional narratives of storytelling, although they do involve themselves with other, less advanced societies and try to prod them towards certain views.<br /> <br /> The first Culture book is about a sworn enemy of the Culture (this is thousands of years before the later Culture book) who has allied with a roughly equiv-tech expansionist Empire who the Culture oppose on ethical grounds, and even he admits that he fights the Culture because of very broad, universal ideological reasons, and the Culture is still a great place to live (he's also kind of a machinophobe, if such a word could exist). Two of the most highly regarded Culture books are about a Culture agent messing around with a society they are trying to prod in a better direction (Player of Games) and an exploration of the psyche and actions of a non-Culture mercenary who is employed by the Culture as an agent in their schemes (Use of Weapons). So the Culture books do very much recognise that the Culture is only really interesting when you put it alongside something else, something the Culture isn't, and see how they interact.<br /> <br /> And there's nothing wrong with putting it alongside Creation, except that Creation simply works on different narrative rules, so there's no real way to do both settings justice IMO. If someone disagrees with that, and wants to discuss how the two settings could slot together and interact then fine! yes! Cool! Let's talk! But I don't think it's unreasonable to want you to, you know, have read a Culture novel and not be trying to poke holes in how it works as a setting without any kind of actual knowledge of that setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":117,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","user":"Solar","date":". 04-23-2015, 10:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T08:00:34-07:00","id":489491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Solar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489413#post489413\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, if none of you have actually read the books then you're best off just, you know, reading one or two of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Rather than just have you describe it again and again, you mean? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Just saying, they may be fantastically written, a phenomenal series of works that changes the face of sci-fi literature forevermore, but you're not doing the best job of selling it to us.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T08:17:31-07:00","id":489501,"title":"","content":"Well, there's nothing that I haven't said about the Culture that isn't true, re. their level of technological advancement or moral framework. But at the same time, as I said, the books aren't really about the Culture in isolation, they're about the Culture fucking around with other peoples.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, I was skeptical that the Culture could work, narratively, when I first was told about the books. Turns out, this is not the case, it actually works really well, though it's very high concept and thoughtful sci-fi. Space battles in the Culture books are things that happen in a few light-seconds of space and are over before an un-enhanced human could even really register they have begun, but they're still exciting, because reading about a Culture warship battling against some equiv-tech enemy vessels is really cool.<br /> <br /> Which is a good point, by the by, the Culture isn't alone at their tech level, nor is it at the top of the pile. But the kind of things that are a threat to the Culture are, like, other versions of the Culture. There's a species called the Gzlit who load their warships with the digitised consciousness of biological crews and run them on stupendously advanced computers , and the Culture believes that this gives them roughly as much capability overall as a Mind-piloted Culture warship. So there is that, if you need something like that to make it seem more palatable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T09:27:50-07:00","id":489578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JayTee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489010#post489010\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's... better, I suppose, but it doesn't really address my current issue with the series. Lemme rephrase the question so I can get a better understanding: What is the main source of conflict in the culture series, and why have the minds not immediately solved it? Assuming that the minds are as intelligent as claimed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Being super intelligent doesn't mean you can perfectly persuade people that you're right, or perfectly implement your ideas on societies that are not receptive to them. <br /> <br /> Being super intelligent doesn't mean that you are immune to your own moral biases.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T10:17:01-07:00","id":489631,"title":"","content":"I feel that Solar has accurately represented the Culture novels, I can't find any fault in Solar's representations of them. In fact, <i>Player of Games</i> in its opening chapters very accurately conveys why a novel set only in the Culture proper would be boring - because nothing really wrong/bad/conflicting ever takes place within the culture. Even when a faction decides to split off from the Culture (like the Zetetic Elench), the Culture is generally, &quot;Well, okay, you're free to go if you'd like. Keep in touch, though, because we still care about you.&quot; The Culture only becomes really interesting when it's dealing with something outside of itself, an <i>Outside Context Problem</i> so to speak (I just started reading <i>Excession </i>and this book is blowing my mind).<br /> <br /> In Excession, the topic of Sublimation is discussed. This is basically Stargate's Ascension. It's transcending material existence to exist as pure consciousness on a &quot;higher plane of existence.&quot; The Culture could Sublimate but chooses not to because, it's noted, that none of the Elder Civilizations which have Sublimated have chosen to deal with harmful hegemonies or save populated worlds which are little too close to supernovae. They're content to remain in transcendent bliss and abandon the lesser forms of life and civilization to cruel chance. The Culture isn't comfortable leading behind the universe in which they do a great deal of good. The Culture is a civilization of Space Bodhisattvas, in this way. They believe in maximizing happiness for all living beings, but they also believe in the freedom to choose. So they'll never <i>force</i> a civilization to change, but they'll encourage it in interesting ways. <i>Player of Games</i> shows a very interesting way the Culture destabilizes an objectively horrible Empire in which rape and slavery are the most mild of their daily atrocities.<br /> <br /> If the Culture were to encounter Creation, their approach would belie their abilities. They never allow lesser civilizations to know the full extent of what they're capable of because they're worried that when the average person learns that an egalitarian utopia is possible, that it exists, and that they're not in one because their leaders are selfish, greedy assholes, they'd revolt. There'd be widespread violence, death and new power structures would emerge. This would create more problems than it would solve. So the Culture would play at money, creating wealth to use in Creation's economies - enough to interact with it, but not so much that they'd be considered wealthy. Once they've inserted themselves into Creation's socio-economic fabric, they'd examine its power structures and see very quickly that power in Creations comes from money and essence. Essence-Users (including the Exalted) tend to make their way into positions of wealth, influence and authority. This entire top-down power structure would stop being effective if <i>everyone</i> could use essence, and so the Culture would find a way to allow everyone to use Essence. It wouldn't necessarily offer that to <i>Creation's</i> average citizen at first, but everyone who is part of the Culture would have that ability.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-23T13:19:08-07:00","id":489774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489631#post489631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Culture were to encounter Creation, their approach would belie their abilities. They never allow lesser civilizations to know the full extent of what they're capable of because they're worried that when the average person learns that an egalitarian utopia is possible, that it exists, and that they're not in one because their leaders are selfish, greedy assholes, they'd revolt. There'd be widespread violence, death and new power structures would emerge. This would create more problems than it would solve. So the Culture would play at money, creating wealth to use in Creation's economies - enough to interact with it, but not so much that they'd be considered wealthy. Once they've inserted themselves into Creation's socio-economic fabric, they'd examine its power structures and see very quickly that power in Creations comes from money and essence. Essence-Users (including the Exalted) tend to make their way into positions of wealth, influence and authority. This entire top-down power structure would stop being effective if <i>everyone</i> could use essence, and so the Culture would find a way to allow everyone to use Essence. It wouldn't necessarily offer that to <i>Creation's</i> average citizen at first, but everyone who is part of the Culture would have that ability.</div> </div> </div> </div>...and that brings us back around to the core problem of trying to mash a high-fantasy world and a hard sci-fi world together.<br /> <br /> In Creation, according to Exalted's default assumptions, not everyone can use Essence because the world was not made with that possibility in mind, literally. The most powerful essence-users are the Primordials. Those beings that made the world, decided the rules that would govern it, and populated it with their progeny and creations decided they weren't interested in competition, so they didn't make any - except the gods, who were bound to obey them. The kind of magic it would take to turn all sentient beings into essence users would be completely unprecedented, if it were possible at all - the Working that made it <i>possible</i> for everyone to learn sorcery would pale in comparison. And even if everyone could use essence, not everyone would be able to use it equally because your ability to wield essence is dependent mostly on how much effort you put into it (leaving aside details of your approach, be it meditation or hard training or persuading large numbers of people to worship you).<br /> <br /> To make the whole thing even remotely plausible, you'd probably have to hack together some kind of motonic physics model of essence that would mesh with real physics instead of the narrative conventions essence follows now. Knowledge of the power of essence would entirely cease to be a sign of your transcendent understanding of the world and instead be something like knowledge of gravity or electromagnetism - a force that anyone can manipulate with the right tools. In short, the Culture's setting assumptions would totally override Exalted's setting assumptions.<br /> <br /> As has been talked about before, you can pick and choose elements from one setting to put in another if you reskin them a bit - I still think Autocthon is very Mind-like - but direct crossovers seem to inevitably result in this unresolvable clash of ideas.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">I also disagree with the assumption that Creation's societies are categorically &quot;lesser&quot; than the Culture. Probably the Minds and Contact humans would think that way, but that doesn't make it less subjective.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 04-23-2015, 09:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T15:19:14-07:00","id":492012,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wulf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=489631#post489631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Culture were to encounter Creation, their approach would belie their abilities. They never allow lesser civilizations to know the full extent of what they're capable of because they're worried that when the average person learns that an egalitarian utopia is possible, that it exists, and that they're not in one because their leaders are selfish, greedy assholes, they'd revolt. There'd be widespread violence, death and new power structures would emerge. This would create more problems than it would solve. So the Culture would play at money, creating wealth to use in Creation's economies - enough to interact with it, but not so much that they'd be considered wealthy. Once they've inserted themselves into Creation's socio-economic fabric, they'd examine its power structures and see very quickly that power in Creations comes from money and essence. Essence-Users (including the Exalted) tend to make their way into positions of wealth, influence and authority. This entire top-down power structure would stop being effective if <i>everyone</i> could use essence, and so the Culture would find a way to allow everyone to use Essence. It wouldn't necessarily offer that to <i>Creation's</i> average citizen at first, but everyone who is part of the Culture would have that ability.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like I said, I feel that people are ignoring two things:<br /> <br /> 1. From Creation's perspective, the Culture's Minds are not substantially more powerful than the Primordials. They pose certain problems (lacking what a Solar would consider a physical body, say, and to the extent that they have any physical presence it is utterly unapproachable), but the Solars have killed things like that repeatedly before. Therefore, any crossover that doesn't allow the Solars to kill at least <i>one</i> Mind before they realize they have to defend themselves is IMHO completely ignoring everything that makes Creation what it is; I don't think it's reasonable to even discuss such a crossover. The question in your write-up is therefore &quot;how does the Culture react when its minds start dying, feeling fear, and so on?&quot;<br /> <br /> 2. On the other hand, from the Culture's perspective? The culture and its minds are explicitly <i>not</i> transcendent. The idea that the Culture could encounter a civilization that looks primitive but which has some technology that even Culture minds are unable to comprehend is not outside of the scope of their genre. (From the Culture's perspective, the Primordials and perhaps the Incarnae are to an extent paradoxes, in that they're transcended beings who have not completely lost interest in the world... although I guess the Incarnae mostly have, now. The Exalted are people who are otherwise primitive but who were given chunks of technology far beyond what even the Culture can begin to analyze. Encountering weird situations like that is, as people have said, what you do when you want an interesting Culture novel.)<br /> <br /> That interpretation fits in with both the assumptions of the Culture novels and with the assumptions of the Exalted. It's the most <i>interesting</i> way to look at it, beyond that. It feels like some people above are saying &quot;no, no, the Culture always wins&quot;, which is definitely not in the books; or even just &quot;Creation is primitive, of course the Culture wins against primitive people&quot;, which is, again, not what Creation would look like to the Culture itself once they encounter Exaltation. In certain specific ways (that is, when it comes to Exaltation), it's reasonable to assume that the 'technology' behind Exaltation vastly exceeds that of the Culture; that's the only way you can really run the two of them side by side and get a meaningful story that respects the assumptions of both.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 07-17-2015, 01:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T16:04:04-07:00","id":492059,"title":"","content":"I don't know if this will help, just to be clear though, on my post, a lot of the point of what I posted at least was *not* exactly that the Minds would for sure instantly solve Creation's problems.<br /> <br /> That all depends on interpretation to a degree. The Great Curse might (in a crossover) be something that they just genuinely couldn't crack or get a handle on - there might be magic that goes &quot;No matter how smart you are and how much you can work out about history, you won't see this in plain sight&quot; or if they suggested solutions, the answer might elude them. For instance. You'd have to decide.<br /> <br /> The point really was that they aren't transcendent intelligences like Primordials or the Neverborn, who are hostile or aggressive to humanity and regard it as at best a resource to be exploited. They're not ones with weird, &quot;alien&quot; or robotic blindspots that prevent them understanding spirituality or ethics or human potential (or potentially understanding Essence).<br /> <br /> They're transhuman intelligences who are kind and well intentioned and have a great understanding of people. They don't blunder in and attack others. They work out what's happening, how the world around them works and adapt to follow suit. And because they don't believe in scarcity or zero sum situations or winner takes all and are genuinely committed to making a better world for humanity, they try and help, without just straight up controlling people to do what they think is right.<br /> <br /> They wouldn't, by their own intention, probably be a foe for the Solars at least to fight. And if they are, chances are you're not necessarily going to be rooting for the Exalted as the champions of humanity against a force that would oppress it (rather the opposite of anything). Without a great deal of saying they can't do what they normally would be expected to do because magic (and if you do this a lot, why include the Minds anyway?) then should achieve a good deal of progress.<br /> <br /> Their levels of intelligence are very consistent with being able to figure out the history of Creation very quickly and they certainly do have a huge amount information on a vast number of cultures from across space in reality for comparison. Many of them (it changes as the Culture books take place at different times in the Culture's timeline) are meant to be civilization scale intelligences equivalent to entire space opera civilizations in their ability.<br /> <br /> To use an (perhaps shaky) analogy, if I said, for some reason, &quot;Mohandas Gandhi suddenly appears in Creation. Except he has the speed of thought many orders of magnitude faster than any human can, and access to as much knowledge and intellectual capacity as an entire far future space opera civilization&quot; and the response was &quot;Well, Gandhi will be all at sea in Creation, as it is immune to his scientific analysis. And the Exalted will totally be able to kill Gandhi before he realises what's up&quot;. Then wouldn't it be like &quot;Seriously (leaving aside the assumptions about why Gandhi wouldn't grok Essence) why are you killing Gandhi again? Are you Tyler Durden? He is a nice man with, in this scenario, to say again, the intellectual and productive capacity of an entire far future space opera civilization at his command.&quot;?<br /> <br /> Although, in a crossover, I could actually see forces like the Deathlords or Fair Folk taking on or trying to suborn the Minds - which could work because magic if you want, and then they might need some magical assistance as a hard counter.<br /> <br /> (If a Mind did die to a Creation born force, my mental image doesn't really involve them feeling fear exactly. More like acting like Hange Zoe does to the Titans).<br /> <br /> As to the actual Culture books, yeah, like others have said the main questions that come up IRC (and you'd be better off looking at synopses of the books for this, as it has been a while) are hard ethical questions on how the Culture interacts with other civilizations and personal level drama between people within Culture societies, and particularly the Culture's malcontents, usually in remote areas away from where the Minds are. They were very compelling books, when I read them, as everyone has said. Banks generally does all this while respecting his premise of including strongly godlike friendly AI.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T16:17:00-07:00","id":492064,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=492059#post492059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(If a Mind did die to a Creation born force, my mental image doesn't really involve them feeling fear exactly. More like acting like Hange Zoe does to the Titans)</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the Minds would feel fear (and be subject to similar things) because the Exalted have powers that say &quot;no, seriously, you feel fear even if it doesn't make sense&quot; and the like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To use an (perhaps shaky) analogy, if I said, for some reason, &quot;Mohandas Gandhi suddenly appears in Creation. Except he has the speed of thought many orders of magnitude faster than any human can, and access to as much knowledge and intellectual capacity as an entire far future space opera civilization&quot; and the response was &quot;Well, Gandhi will be all at sea in Creation, as it is immune to his scientific analysis. And the Exalted will totally be able to kill Gandhi before he realises what's up&quot;. Then wouldn't it be like &quot;Seriously (leaving aside the assumptions about why Gandhi wouldn't grok Essence) why are you killing Gandhi again? Are you Tyler Durden? He is a nice man with, in this scenario, to say again, the intellectual and productive capacity of an entire far future space opera civilization at his command.&quot;? </div> </div> </div>I think that the Exalted <i>could</i> kill Culture Minds; and given the diversity of Exalted and the way they tend to be malcontents, I suspect eventually one of them would actually do it.<br /> <br /> A big part of how the Exalted work (especially Solars) is that they can drag supposedly untouchable beings down into the dirt and beat them up. To me, the most interesting part of a Culture / Exalted clash is how the Culture reacts to that and to the realization that Exaltation can (sometimes, situationally) negate its advantages. It will probably <i>think</i> it can do what you're saying it can do and stay hands-off as the above-the-fray bodhisattva bringing enlightenment and peace to this savage world. But it can't, not if you're treating the Exalted as anything like what they're supposed to be -- if they meddle in Creation, eventually they're going to step on the toes of an Exalt who gets lucky and manages to actually hurt the Culture itself in some appreciable sense (probably, like I said, by successfully killing a Mind.) The real question is how the Culture reacts at that point -- once they realize that they're dealing with a world where individual people can theoretically hold that level of unaccountable power.<br /> <br /> I also think that this is part of why the Minds' efforts to analyze Creation wouldn't warn them of what they're up against, at least not initially. Figuring out the direction of an entire civilization is much easier than predicting what one guy will do; a world where random malcontents can suddenly be endowed with this transcendent 'technology' that lets them theoretically fight back against any degree of force is something that will go outside anyone's normal ability to predict the future through any degree of statistical inference. Even if they do everything completely right and are utterly careful, eventually someone like the Invincible Sword Princess will flip out (possibly because of Limit Break) and cause problems for them.<br /> <br /> (I don't think that it would come to the point of an outright <i>war</i>, of course, not if the Exalted are late-second-age default setting Exalted, because the Exalted aren't organized enough for that. It would be like the Culture was a first world nation trying to intervene in a war-torn third-world hellhole only to find out that some of the people living there have personal nuclear arsenals. Or something like that.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 04-25-2015, 07:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T17:02:58-07:00","id":492101,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=492064#post492064\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Minds would feel fear (and be subject to similar things) because the Exalted have powers that say &quot;no, seriously, you feel fear even if it doesn't make sense&quot; and the like.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess that's pretty possible, depending on how far you want to go into er... Mind control territory with Exalted magic. But I would say, if that happens, then that's seems definitely Creation doing weird mind control magic stuff to the Culture, not really the way the Minds would react &quot;naturally&quot;. It seems that far from how Minds in general, would process that situation, although others who've read the books more recently might differ in their assessment of this though, and the Minds do vary in their personality. I don't know that they actually even have any great fear of death as such, based on the books. <br /> <br /> This may not quite be your point, but remember, we're not projecting from them being Primordial like beings who greatly fear and are disturbed by the idea of personal ending, and find it unprecedented and shocking. They're profoundly not Primordials, in how they think and act and especially their relationship as effectively the main champions and leaders of Culture &quot;humanity&quot;. Probably a number of Minds have fallen to the Culture's struggles in the past, so I don't know if they'd be that surprised that it could happen or necessarily that it would trigger any waves of cultural angst or self examination or panic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T21:04:01-07:00","id":492274,"title":"","content":"I admit, I am rather intrigued by what would happen when a Culture Mind is subjected to Husband-Seducing Demon Dance and becomes completely infatuated with a Solar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-25T21:23:27-07:00","id":492306,"title":"","content":"You could experiment with your players finding I AM from DotFA, who is basically a Mind in all but name. Might be interesting to see the results.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2034-jaytee","name":"JayTee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2034_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-26T01:59:03-07:00","id":492442,"title":"","content":"Man, just waded into this conversation.. I LOVE the culture book and also Exalted.<br /> I think this was said before - but this scenario really depends HEAVILY on which &quot;story physics&quot; are considered real?<br /> Is this an exalted story, or a culture novel?<br /> <br /> My personal take would be that Creation and the Exalted would definitely be an &quot;outside context problem&quot;: really, its a world that seems to get close to being a simulation of a real world but then gets all the little things wrong - flat, surrounded by strange chaotic dimensions, etc. This would work best if Creation was not just &quot;oh, another world we've found&quot; but rather the a culture Mind discovering a loophole in physics and entering a strange new dimension (heck, hyperspace sorta works by skimming between strange dimensions).<br /> <br /> Like the return of Authochton - which way to we want to play this? Is it a couple of Special Circumstances operatives being infiltrated into creation to try and work out what the hell they found, Or is this a circle of exalts who manage to break out and are now interacting with spaceships and hi tech?<br /> <br /> Either way, this would be a fun story I think. The Culture novels are interesting because its never about the &quot;tech&quot;. Its always about personalities and their reactions. About the specific operative and their internal response to the situation - and what they think is the appropriate response. The SC agents are almost like Exalted - they can &quot;do&quot; pretty much anything, but its the fallout of those activities that makes the novels interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/37-dionysus","name":"Dionysus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar37_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"104"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-26T02:02:11-07:00","id":492443,"title":"","content":"I could so see this novel <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> A mind discovers a new edge case of physics and makes a &quot;portal&quot; into creation. Sends a few drones (human level AI) in to scout around. Get shocked when they are subsumed and taken (siddies bring them into fate, and then either kill or &quot;turn&quot; them).<br /> <br /> Decides, &quot;nope nope, too dangerous, lets stop this experimentation&quot;. And then gets shocked when a few years later the portal is reopened from the other side and these apparent primitives come through. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/37-dionysus","name":"Dionysus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar37_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"104"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-26T09:49:09-07:00","id":492666,"title":"","content":"I agree with what you said in the last paragraph two posts above, Dyonisus.<br /> <br /> The readon why the novels made such an impression on me is the fate of their protagonists - Gurgeh, Horza, the SC agent with the nuke in her head -.<br /> <br /> Blessed with super-natural gifts, talents, ressources and insight, offered a life of leisure, comfort and enlightenment, and yet doomed - similar to Greek mythological heroes but closer to us in some ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5810-debana-men","name":"Debana Men","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-27T02:59:00-07:00","id":493404,"title":"","content":"All other considerations aside, I'll point out that Exalted already has a concept of automaton intelligences that are not susceptible to emotion-based effects.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-27T22:36:56-07:00","id":494416,"title":"","content":"I've been working on a character concept over the last few days that is heavily influenced by the Culture Novels. A former Lookshy city planner, Chosen by the Sun, who becomes obsessed with finding out more about this primordial &quot;Machine God&quot; referenced in some incomplete texts on subjects he can't quite wrap his head around. He's has a double-motivation: On the surface he genuinely wishes to be of the greatest possible benefit to humankind and, to achieve that aim, he wants to build a new kind of civilization that will maximize human happiness. This is an enormous task that will have him questioning everything from the socio-economic power structures of the world to the role of the Exalted themselves. He would also label Hell, the Underworld, the Wyld and even Autochthonia as Outside Context Problems. The Loom of Fate would deeply disturb him (&quot;What do you mean it governs causality and no one knows how it works or what to do if it breaks?!&quot;)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T10:00:53-07:00","id":494978,"title":"","content":"My main question is, what allows him to think so abstractly? Frankly, most people--including very powerful people--are enmeshed in getting things done on a more local or regional level - to benefit the people they actually see day-to-day, rather than some nebulous population beyond the horizon.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure if it'd actually work in 3e and it's based off a huge set of assumptions in 2e that I'm hesitant to call kind of Sue-ish, but that's just how it feels to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot; --TheDementedOne<br /> <br /> &quot;Happiness is <i>very</i> brittle and short-lived in the Exalted community, because <i>ressentiment</i> is our cultural touchstone.&quot; --Gayo","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3420-zelbinnean","name":"Zelbinnean","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5884"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-28T10:40:16-07:00","id":495001,"title":"","content":"Good point. I think I'll scale-back his concept to being of the greatest benefit to Lookshy with the idea that he will expand that compassion outside its borders once he realizes that people from all over Creation aren't all that different.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\"><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">\u263e</span></span></b></span></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/438-wulf","name":"wulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar438_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T20:50:19-07:00","id":1296891,"title":"","content":"Can I give my 2 cents? <br /> <br /> I think the most dangerous part of the Exalted Host, isn't because of their normal power at creation levels. Its their versatility. If you were to import a solar exalt into Modern Creation, they will be able to develop charms based on cars, computers, guns, and planes. Sure, maybe they're just not cut out for it. Maybe they're the equivalent of that old grandma who barely understand internet explorer, or that man who's afraid of planes. But most will, and most will understand. They'll start with excellencies, allowing them to match the best mortal programmer or sharp shooter. Then they get better. If they invest enough effort, they'll be able to drive a motorcycle through the DMZ, or snipe a man from the moon, or hack into nuclear silos and use the nukes for themselves.<br /> <br /> You can never truly be safe from an exalted. You can never just lock them up into a cage, pat your back, and then walk away. The sidereal will just use avoidance kata. The solar will use lock-opening touch. The Lunar will either buff their strength to bend the bars, or develop a dexterity charm that will let them squeeze through the gaps. The Dragonblooded will dissolve into either wind or water, then reform on the other side.<br /> <br /> You cannot build a wall, and then just say 'ok, we're safe now', and laugh at the primitives hammering on the other side. The solar will develop Wall-evading technique. The Lunar will walk through the realm of dreams and exit on the other side. The Dragonblooded will hammer on the stone force it to give way. The Sidereal will have always been on the same side as you. No way to run. No way to be safe. No way to look at them, and say 'Yeah, there's no way they're going to get out of that'. Because some will, and some will survive, and some will come after you. Then you'll lose. <br /> <br /> The only way to permanently be safe from the exalted host is to kill them all, and if you fail, and some humans are left alive, I guarantee you that the solar exaltations will flock to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T21:52:51-07:00","id":1296906,"title":"","content":"The big thing is the Culture generally assumes that a technocratic, perfectly raitonal planning thing could mange people to a point ot bring about utopia. That it's just a matter that folks in power aren't competent enough and those who are competent are not in power.<br /> <br /> A big quesiton you have to ask is whether that is actually the case. While there was a lot of Solar utopia-stuff pushed in 2e, a big thing to remember is that ultimately, whether the super-competent and powerful person were in charge is not actually a big problem with human history. And in fact, a lot of times, folks who are very competent and in power, but who also think soemthing is a Really Good Idea on Paper have created massive human cost as a result. Some of the greatest human disastors in history could, arguably, be described in context of folks doing the reasonable/systematic thing and finding what they're tyring to fix well, isn't. This is the thesis of books like <b>Seeing Like a State</b> as a note, which is a big influence in 3e.<br /> <br /> This is actually a big factor of the Culture and the series that has to kind of be considered. Socieites might <i>not</i> be something that you just can brute force your way and predict outcomes form and contorl those outcomes like an engineering problem. They instead might be more dynamical in their nature. That is to say, a dynamical system is one with determined results, but unpredictable outcomes. There's no Laplace's Demon to this that you just need to be smart enough about. There are systems that <i>at best</i> you can get probabalistic outcomes for, and no matter what, that's all you get. ANd sociites are ones which have ludicrous amounts of widgets to fuck any of that up, all of them determined dynamical systems. This isn't just quantum mechanics as a note. There are macroscopic systems that behave like this, in ecology, psychology, and computer science to name a few.<br /> <br /> So looping this to Exalted, a big thing to ask is whether or not Solar indivdiual competence can generate the things the Culture has. I, personally, don't even think the Culture is possible. And even if it were, Solar Exalted, who are ultiamtely very competent humans with not a lot of restraint to stop them when they get a &quot;bright idea&quot; to very competently execute, are probably amongst the <i>worse</i> people to possibly try to implement something like it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T23:09:25-07:00","id":1296916,"title":"","content":"ok...<br /> <br /> I'm going to ignore the solar's personalities and character. Its irrelevant for now. <br /> <br /> Solars. Bureaucracy supernal. Lore supernal. Essence 5. Perfection. <br /> <br /> Solars can block anything. Using anything. They can pick up a spoon, look upon a full on blast of gridfire, then parry it away. A solar can be eating dinner, have a nuclear missile explode 10 meters away, and use the fork he is using to turn back the shockwave and radiation. Solars can withstand anything. Adamant skin technique allows him to withstand any blow. From a rpg to the punch of a giant 3 yeddim wide. <br /> <br /> Solars can shoot perfectly. If they can perceive it, they can hit it. Accuracy without distance means that if they were to stand on one end of the solar system, and see a pebble floating on the other end, they can pick up a pistol, aim, pull the trigger, and hit it. Without fail. Because that's what solars do. They do something perfectly, with the end result already confirmed and spoken for. <br /> <br /> Its not just super competent person being in charge. Its also super competent person being in charge, and that person's power flowing from 'peak human' to 'stepping out of the mists of legend'.<br /> <br /> No matter how good a bureaucrat Stalin can be (and he wasn't really one), he can't fix everything. If a man in the western section lies and says that the amount of steel is rising, when instead it is actually falling, due to his fear of being punished for laziness, Stalin can't do anything about it. Same with feudal kings. King Richard can't deal with the fact that one of his tax collectors have been overcharging, because he doesn't have the available information. Or a corrupt bureaucrat that's subtly pushing his family's appeals and applications to the front of any line. Or a bribed crony intentionally misplacing a report of corruption that was meant for the big boss.<br /> <br /> Solars can make their bureaucracies move faster by an order of magnitude. Solars can use their superhuman skills to purge the bureaucracy of any incompetence and corruption. Solars can use their superhuman power to make their organizations immune to corruption. Solars can use their training charms into making ordinary mortals into the best organizers and bureaucrats in the world. <br /> <br /> I think that Solars, with enough power, can successfully run Communist states, Laissez-Faire, or any type of government, as long as they're willing to put the effort in doing so. If they have the idea to attempt something? They will succeed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T23:25:33-07:00","id":1296919,"title":"","content":"This is a pretty significant thread necromancy, dude - I'm pretty sure some of these people don't participate on the forums anymore.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=1296916#post1296916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going to ignore the solar's personalities and character. Its irrelevant for now.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is never going to be true.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=1296916#post1296916\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Solars. Bureaucracy supernal. Lore supernal. Essence 5. Perfection.</div> </div> </div> </div>1) You can't have two Supernals.<br /> 2) Being Essence 5 makes your Supernal irrelevant.<br /> 3) If that was all you needed for &quot;perfection,&quot; then why was there ever a Usurpation?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-14T23:38:05-07:00","id":1296922,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TheCountAlucard</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=1296919#post1296919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a pretty significant thread necromancy, dude - I'm pretty sure some of these people don't participate on the forums anymore.<br /> <br /> <br /> This is never going to be true.<br /> <br /> 1) You can't have two Supernals.<br /> 2) Being Essence 5 makes your Supernal irrelevant.<br /> 3) If that was all you needed for &quot;perfection,&quot; then why was there ever a Usurpation?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok. Fine. <br /> <br /> When I was saying supernals, I meant either one. But biased towards Bureaucracy. <br /> <br /> Essence 5 is for extra power. Speed the wheels gets stronger the higher your essence. <br /> <br /> &quot;Perfection&quot;. <br /> <br /> \u200bI was talking to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar353_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"353\" data-vbnamecard=\"353\">Blaque</a> . When I say perfection, I meant that if a solar puts an organizational goal or has a plan for his government, he's going to do it, he's going to carry it out, and he's going to succeed. Whether its 'put all these orphans in good families' or 'perfectly distribute welfare across the nation, so that none go hungry or without medicine' or 'redistribute supplies because this state is communist'.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T02:08:32-07:00","id":1296935,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=1296906#post1296906\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The big thing is the Culture generally assumes that a technocratic, perfectly raitonal planning thing could mange people to a point ot bring about utopia. That it's just a matter that folks in power aren't competent enough and those who are competent are not in power.<br /> <br /> A big quesiton you have to ask is whether that is actually the case. While there was a lot of Solar utopia-stuff pushed in 2e, a big thing to remember is that ultimately, whether the super-competent and powerful person were in charge is not actually a big problem with human history. And in fact, a lot of times, folks who are very competent and in power, but who also think soemthing is a Really Good Idea on Paper have created massive human cost as a result. Some of the greatest human disastors in history could, arguably, be described in context of folks doing the reasonable/systematic thing and finding what they're tyring to fix well, isn't. This is the thesis of books like <b>Seeing Like a State</b> as a note, which is a big influence in 3e.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's complete nonsense. First, the Culture isn't a complete utopia. I mean... things like Meatfucker exist. Wars still happen, and people die in them. It's just a lot better than things are today.<br /> <br /> Yes, &quot;Utopia&quot; in the sense of &quot;the best possible system&quot; isn't possible because there's no clearly-defined &quot;best&quot;; but throughout history, the human condition has varied greatly from time period to time period and from place to place; and while there has been significant random variation, I think it's clearly absurd to suggest that it is <i>all</i> random. Some things clearly lead to better standards of living, longer lifespans, and so on.<br /> <br /> That means that it's reasonable to conclude that things could be better. The progress we've made might be meandering and often come at great costs; there's a lot of room to argue over what has actually worked or what the actual causes are, and so on. But it proves that progress <i>is</i> possible. And while technological advancement (including super-AI overlords or whatever) certainly isn't always going to automatically improve things forever, the fact that we already come so far is sufficient reason to conclude that it is at least possible for us to make a better world. More specifically, technological advancement is capable of changing the core underlying assumptions and interactions that have lead to problems in the past - diseases and famines still happen, for instance, but we have far better tools to control or limit them today.<br /> <br /> We don't need perfectly-rational AIs who can predict every possible outcome. We just need to be able to iteratively improve on what we have. And there's no reason to think that that's impossible. Culture Minds are <i>not</i> perfect (and to an extent they're a stand-in for vague future technology that we can't, of course, perfectly predict today.) But I think it's silly to suggest that using tools like that couldn't improve the human condition at all, and nothing about that degree of improvement strikes me as so implausible as to warrant this sort of scorn.<br /> <br /> Beyond that, while I get that you're looking down at the overly-simplistic assumptions people make when talking about what a super-smart AI or the like could do... just throwing up your hands and saying &quot;it's complex and therefore can never be solved better than we've solved it today!&quot; is just as overly-simplistic.<br /> <br /> I think a more reasonable criticism is that the tools that allow people to drastically change the human condition in sci-fi novels like those could be just as easily used to change it for the worse - historical progress is no predictor of future performance! - and that that's probably more worth worrying about given the state of the world today. But &quot;a better society is impossible because things are complicated, maaan&quot; strikes me as lazy thinking, too.<br /> <br /> And &quot;yeah, but think of all the times when people tried to make society better and then BAD STUFF happened!&quot; is even worse. Like... yeah, we're well aware things can go horrifically wrong even with smart people and the best of intentions. That's not a reason to stop pushing for things to be better, not when the day-to-day status quo causes more pain and suffering and death than anything else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T07:19:31-07:00","id":1296971,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar517_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"517\" data-vbnamecard=\"517\">Aquillion</a> MInd, in theory if we had super-intelligent AIs to account for every-fuck up a human can introduce, it probably would work. I am skeptical it ever gets there because you still have not-perfect people implementing things and so you are, as you note, just as likely to fuck things up. Especially since no system is free from human biases and issues therein. Note I don't disagree we should make progress. I just think that human systems are complex enough that such progress is oging ot be hard, have a lot of hiccups and super-strong centralized authority wrested on individuals is kind of asking for it to not work. I think my skepticism of the possiblity of something liket he Culture is I think a society will blow itself up before the AI goes on trying to make everyone's lives better.<br /> <br /> I'm also not saying that we can't improve things. Just that there are systems that as we can gather, <i>are not deterministic</i> and so need to be approached as such. I'm noting that what is needed is probably different conceptions than typically is overly applied phsyics or material engineering solutions to a lot of problems. And that things probably <i>will</i> break somewhere, and that folks should expect to have contingencies when they do. A lot of issues I think have in human history, even with policies I agree with which have had downstream consequences, often happen to cause problems since there's a problem pointed out that the implementors think will just fix itself. Or they say they need the thing implemented first...and then don't get to the Part Two of actually fixing issues.<br /> <br /> You're mischarecterizing me as a skeptic of societal progress because I am skeptical that something like I understand the Culture is a way to get there or that it is ever possible due to well, it's hard to get there. My issue is that society isn't just an engineering problem, or at least one that short of AI is fixable, and I'm skeptical we can ever get to the AI without I dunno, Facebook or Chiense Social Credit first, because the values of a system's creators aren't extractable and so I'm not so clear how we get to soemthing like the stuff in the Culture.<br /> <br /> It's also, note, why I say that even granting the Culture being possible, <i>Solars</i> probably can't do it. Because while I can grant a detached AI that somehow manages to surpass it's creator's own biases for a greater good (defined as peaceful, prosperous, post-resources carcity I am guessing) probably, Solar Exlted emphatically <i>aren't</i> those and will despite their high competence often probably hit a lot of the &quot;There's not an objective right way to do X without hurting someone&quot; things we have to deal with in policy making today. And being people, the Solar's biases are going to affect who she decides is the cost in any cost-benefit analysis.<br /> <br /> (I'll also note I only know about the series through secondhand, as I have not personally read it. But I have seen it explianed and discussed and it kind of hits up against what I have been reading elsewhere of late.)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12952\" data-vbnamecard=\"12952\">Accelerator</a> Solar Bureaucracy is really good at managing systems, finding people to break those systems, and knowing how you can implement them. But it's notable that actually as written, it <i>doesn't</i> let you perfectly implement any program you want. You are probably the best at wiedling bureacracies to be able to implement policies you want and such...as long as you also make sure the people below you implement them. I think it's notable a good chunk of the Solar Charmset is about rooting out corruption, lazy people, and all that. And that even then, you'll note there's no &quot;Perfectly solve problem&quot; Charms. Just getting things inhumanely good at it.<br /> <br /> An issue of why something like Brasilia is a shitty city to live in or that the Five Pests Campaign was a disastor wasn't because the folks inovlved weren't able to implement their visions. It's that hteir visions were shitty to begin with. And to my reading, Solar Bureacracy doesn't actually stop you from doing that. You have to do the research and legwork on your own to find out downstream effects, and it's notable that nothing in the Solar Charmset does that perfectly. Very good, sure. But that you know how to eliminate all sparrows ever doesn't mean you even considered what the downstream effects of that are. Those are different questions and often people implementing sweeping policies like that don't ask them all. And nothing about Solar might in that sense really like, gives them that for free.<br /> <br /> As noted, if their magic did, they'd of not had an Usurpation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T07:21:16-07:00","id":1296972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/479955-the-culture?p=1296922#post1296922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok. Fine.<br /> <br /> When I was saying supernals, I meant either one. But biased towards Bureaucracy.<br /> <br /> Essence 5 is for extra power. Speed the wheels gets stronger the higher your essence.<br /> <br /> &quot;Perfection&quot;.<br /> <br /> \u200bI was talking to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar353_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"353\" data-vbnamecard=\"353\">Blaque</a> . When I say perfection, I meant that if a solar puts an organizational goal or has a plan for his government, he's going to do it, he's going to carry it out, and he's going to succeed. Whether its 'put all these orphans in good families' or 'perfectly distribute welfare across the nation, so that none go hungry or without medicine' or 'redistribute supplies because this state is communist'.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just to respond to this bit here, I am of a view that they actually won't. Or at least, that those specific issues are actually mutli-part. The problem with say, the Cultural Revolution wasn't that Mao had bad Bureaucracy. It was very successful at many of its multiple policy implementations. That success often was what was the problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-15T08:07:50-07:00","id":1296991,"title":"","content":"I have to say something about the idea that minds all somwhow all-knowing and all-adapting. First thing, the Culture Minds have been fooled and blindsided by non-hyperintelligent minds several times (Hidrogen Sonata, Use of Weapons, Matter), and have had a hard time facing other cultures with similar thecnological weapons but no minds (Consider Phlebas and such). In the world of the Culture, being hyperintelligent is great, but is not omniscience, even more so with the ethical restrictions minds have for completely simulating another intelligence's thought-processs (where is the point where a simulation is so complex and complete that it has rights?)<br /> <br /> Also, they cannot analyze even with normal universe situations instantly. A culture vessal took years to parse all of Earth circa 1970 (The State of the Art). And they cannot adapt instantly to knowledge outside the rules of what they know of physics (Excession), in fact they still didn't get their tech for the previewed future. <br /> <br /> Having said that, Creation is in a sense potentially post-scarcity. All is made of essence, and essence flows through all of creation. Minerals are replenished in mines due to the workings of the Loom of Fate and Gods and such, Elements are created on the poles constantly, and so on. And also there is the possibility of creating something out of the wyld, even something as big as Creation, and the wyld if infinite. It has been said that the machinery of Creation is made to create artificially limited resources to mortals to get better prayer-value for the Primordials, and now the Gods. <br /> <br /> Supplanting the gods in yu-shan and reworking the machinery to serve mankind, along with N/A marvels created by the solars would make a post-scarcity society. Making this happen, and making it correctly, would be hard as hell, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1524-pcontop","name":"pcontop","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1524_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}